/* Câu trả lời mẫu IELTS Speaking (tự biên soạn) */
(function (g) { g.SPEAK_SAMPLES = Object.assign(g.SPEAK_SAMPLES || {}, {
  /* ===== Học tập ===== */
  "Do you work or are you a student?": { a: "I'm a full-time student at the moment. I'm in my third year of a business degree, so most of my week revolves around lectures and group projects. That said, I also do a bit of freelance translation at weekends, which helps me cover my living costs and gives me a taste of real working life.", vocab: [["revolve around", "xoay quanh"], ["cover my living costs", "trang trải chi phí sinh hoạt"], ["get a taste of", "được trải nghiệm thử"]] },
  "What subject did you enjoy most at school, and why?": { a: "Without a doubt, it was geography. I think it was because it combined so many things I was curious about, from climate and landscapes to how people actually live in different parts of the world. Our teacher used to bring in maps and photos from her own travels, which really brought the subject to life.", vocab: [["without a doubt", "chắc chắn, không nghi ngờ gì"], ["be curious about", "tò mò về"], ["bring something to life", "làm cho điều gì trở nên sinh động"]] },
  "Do you prefer studying alone or with other people?": { a: "Well, it depends on what I'm doing. When I need to memorise things or write an essay, I definitely prefer working on my own because I can concentrate better. But when I'm revising for exams, studying in a small group is really useful, since we can quiz each other and fill in the gaps in our knowledge.", vocab: [["work on my own", "làm việc một mình"], ["quiz each other", "đố nhau, kiểm tra lẫn nhau"], ["fill in the gaps", "lấp đầy những lỗ hổng (kiến thức)"]] },
  "Describe a teacher who has had a strong influence on you.": { a: "I'd like to talk about Mr Hai, who was my literature teacher in high school. He taught us Vietnamese literature for three years, from grade ten to grade twelve, and I have to say he completely changed the way I looked at the subject.\n\nBefore I joined his class, I thought literature was just about learning model essays by heart and repeating them in exams. Mr Hai, however, had a very different approach. He was quite a quiet man, but he had a dry sense of humour and an incredible passion for books. Instead of giving us the answers, he would ask us open-ended questions and let us argue with each other. Sometimes he even played the devil's advocate on purpose, just to see whether we could defend our opinions.\n\nWhat I remember most clearly is one afternoon when I handed in an essay that I'd basically copied from a guidebook. He didn't scold me in front of the class. Instead, he asked me to stay behind and told me, quite gently, that he wanted to hear my voice, not someone else's. I felt really embarrassed at the time, but that conversation was a real turning point for me.\n\nThe reason he influenced me so much is that he taught me to think for myself. From then on, I started reading more widely and writing what I genuinely believed, even if it wasn't the 'safe' answer. That habit has stayed with me ever since, and it has helped me enormously at university, where critical thinking is valued far more than memorisation. Looking back, I'm incredibly grateful that I ended up in his class.", vocab: [["learn something by heart", "học thuộc lòng"], ["a dry sense of humour", "khiếu hài hước kiểu tỉnh bơ"], ["play the devil's advocate", "cố tình phản biện để thử thách lập luận"], ["a turning point", "bước ngoặt"]] },
  "Should university education be free for everyone?": { a: "I think it's a very appealing idea in principle, because it would give talented young people from poorer backgrounds an equal shot at success. In countries like Germany, free tuition seems to work reasonably well. However, I'm not entirely convinced it's realistic everywhere. Universities are expensive to run, so if students don't pay, taxpayers have to foot the bill, and that money might be needed for schools or hospitals. Perhaps a fairer compromise would be generous scholarships and low-interest loans, so that cost is never the only thing standing between a capable student and a degree.", vocab: [["an equal shot at", "cơ hội ngang bằng để"], ["foot the bill", "chi trả, gánh chi phí"], ["a fairer compromise", "một giải pháp dung hòa công bằng hơn"]] },
  "How has technology changed the way students learn?": { a: "Quite dramatically, I'd say. The most obvious change is access to information: students no longer have to rely solely on textbooks, because they can watch lectures from top universities or look up almost anything in seconds. Online platforms also let people learn at their own pace, which is great for those who need extra time. On the other hand, there's a downside. Many students get easily distracted by notifications, and some rely on AI tools to do their thinking for them. So technology is a powerful tool, but it really depends on whether students use it wisely.", vocab: [["rely solely on", "chỉ dựa vào"], ["learn at their own pace", "học theo tốc độ của riêng mình"], ["get easily distracted", "dễ bị phân tâm"]] },
  "What makes a good teacher, in your opinion?": { a: "For me, the most important quality is genuine enthusiasm for the subject, because passion tends to be contagious. If a teacher clearly loves what they're teaching, students are far more likely to engage. Beyond that, I think good teachers are patient and can explain difficult ideas in a simple way. A teacher I had at school, for instance, would always use everyday examples to explain abstract concepts in physics. Of course, subject knowledge matters too, but I'd argue that being able to connect with students on a personal level is what really sets great teachers apart.", vocab: [["be contagious", "dễ lan tỏa"], ["connect with someone on a personal level", "gắn kết với ai ở mức độ cá nhân"], ["set someone apart", "làm ai nổi bật, khác biệt"]] },

  /* ===== Công nghệ ===== */
  "How often do you use your mobile phone?": { a: "Honestly, far more than I should. It's usually the first thing I check in the morning and the last thing I look at before bed. I use it for everything, from messaging friends to paying for coffee. Recently, though, I've been trying to cut down on screen time by switching it to silent mode when I'm studying.", vocab: [["far more than I should", "nhiều hơn mức nên"], ["cut down on", "cắt giảm"], ["screen time", "thời gian dùng thiết bị điện tử"]] },
  "Which apps do you use most every day?": { a: "I'd say the ones I use most are Zalo and Messenger, because that's how I keep in touch with family and friends. Apart from that, I spend a fair amount of time on a language-learning app, since I try to do a short lesson every day to keep my streak going. And of course, I check Google Maps whenever I'm heading somewhere new.", vocab: [["keep in touch with", "giữ liên lạc với"], ["a fair amount of", "khá nhiều"], ["keep my streak going", "duy trì chuỗi ngày liên tục"]] },
  "Is there any technology you could not live without?": { a: "I'd have to say my laptop. I know it sounds a bit dramatic, but almost all of my coursework, my notes and even my part-time work are on it. A few months ago it broke down for three days, and I felt completely lost. That experience made me realise just how dependent I've become on it.", vocab: [["sound a bit dramatic", "nghe có vẻ hơi phóng đại"], ["break down", "bị hỏng"], ["be dependent on", "phụ thuộc vào"]] },
  "Describe a piece of technology that you find very useful.": { a: "The piece of technology I want to talk about is my pair of noise-cancelling headphones. They might sound like a luxury, but honestly, they've become one of the most useful things I own.\n\nI started using them about two years ago, when I moved into a shared flat near my university. The flat was on a busy street, and there was constant noise from motorbikes, street vendors and, to be honest, my flatmates as well. I was finding it almost impossible to focus on my assignments, so after a lot of research, I decided to save up and buy a decent pair. They weren't cheap, but I thought of it as an investment.\n\nThese days I use them in all sorts of situations. When I'm studying, I switch on the noise-cancelling mode and listen to some instrumental music, and it's amazing how quickly I can get into a state of deep concentration. I also wear them on long bus journeys, so I can listen to podcasts or English lessons without being bothered by the traffic. And when I need a break, they're perfect for unwinding with my favourite albums.\n\nThe main reason they're so useful to me is that they give me control over my environment. Before I had them, I was easily distracted and often stressed, but now I can create my own quiet space wherever I am. My grades actually improved in the semester after I bought them, and I don't think that's a coincidence. Of course, I try not to wear them all the time, because I don't want to shut out the world completely, but overall they've made a huge difference to my productivity and my peace of mind.", vocab: [["save up", "tiết kiệm tiền (để mua gì)"], ["a state of deep concentration", "trạng thái tập trung cao độ"], ["unwind", "thư giãn"], ["peace of mind", "sự an tâm, thanh thản"]] },
  "Do you think people rely too much on technology nowadays?": { a: "To some extent, yes. Many people can't navigate without a map app or remember a single phone number, which suggests we've handed over some basic skills to our devices. I also think constant reliance on smartphones can damage our attention spans. Having said that, I wouldn't call all dependence negative. Technology lets doctors diagnose diseases earlier and allows families to stay connected across continents. So the problem isn't really technology itself, but the lack of balance. If we use it as a tool rather than a crutch, I think the benefits clearly outweigh the drawbacks.", vocab: [["to some extent", "ở một mức độ nào đó"], ["attention span", "khả năng duy trì sự chú ý"], ["use something as a crutch", "dựa dẫm vào điều gì"], ["outweigh the drawbacks", "lớn hơn những bất lợi"]] },
  "How might artificial intelligence change the job market?": { a: "I think AI is likely to reshape the job market quite significantly. Repetitive tasks, such as data entry or basic customer service, are already being automated, so some of those roles may disappear. On the other hand, new jobs will probably emerge, like people who train, monitor or audit AI systems. Personally, I believe the biggest change will be within existing jobs rather than jobs vanishing altogether. A lawyer or a designer, for example, might use AI to handle routine work and focus on more creative or strategic tasks. The real challenge is making sure workers have the chance to retrain.", vocab: [["reshape the job market", "định hình lại thị trường lao động"], ["repetitive tasks", "công việc lặp đi lặp lại"], ["vanish altogether", "biến mất hoàn toàn"], ["retrain", "đào tạo lại (kỹ năng mới)"]] },
  "Should children be allowed to use smartphones?": { a: "I think it depends largely on the child's age and how the phone is used. For young children, I'd say it's better to delay owning a smartphone, because they can easily become addicted to games or exposed to inappropriate content. For teenagers, though, a phone can be genuinely useful, both for safety and for learning. My younger cousin, for instance, uses hers to look up homework questions and contact her parents. So rather than a complete ban, I'd favour clear rules, like screen-time limits and parental controls, so children learn to use technology responsibly.", vocab: [["become addicted to", "bị nghiện"], ["inappropriate content", "nội dung không phù hợp"], ["parental controls", "tính năng kiểm soát của phụ huynh"]] },

  /* ===== Môi trường ===== */
  "Is there much pollution where you live?": { a: "Unfortunately, yes. I live in Hanoi, and air pollution has become a serious problem, especially in winter, when the smog can be quite thick. On bad days, I check an air-quality app before going out and wear a mask. Noise pollution is also an issue, because the streets are packed with motorbikes from early morning until late at night.", vocab: [["air pollution", "ô nhiễm không khí"], ["thick smog", "khói mù dày đặc"], ["be packed with", "chật kín, đầy ắp"]] },
  "Do you do anything to protect the environment?": { a: "I try to do my bit, although I'm far from perfect. For example, I always carry a reusable water bottle and a cloth bag, so I don't need to buy plastic ones. I also separate my rubbish at home. They're small steps, but I think if enough people make these changes, they can really add up.", vocab: [["do my bit", "góp phần nhỏ của mình"], ["far from perfect", "còn xa mới hoàn hảo"], ["add up", "cộng dồn lại thành đáng kể"]] },
  "Do you prefer the countryside or the city?": { a: "At this stage of my life, I'd probably choose the city. There are far more job opportunities, and I love having cafés, cinemas and bookshops on my doorstep. That said, whenever I visit my grandparents in the countryside, I really appreciate the fresh air and the slower pace of life. Maybe I'll move somewhere quieter when I'm older.", vocab: [["on my doorstep", "ngay gần nhà"], ["the slower pace of life", "nhịp sống chậm hơn"], ["at this stage of my life", "ở giai đoạn này của cuộc đời"]] },
  "Describe a natural place you have visited that impressed you.": { a: "The place I'd like to describe is Ha Long Bay, in the north-east of Vietnam. It's famous for its thousands of limestone islands rising out of emerald-green water, and it's a UNESCO World Heritage Site, so I'm sure many people have heard of it.\n\nI went there about two summers ago with my family. We'd been planning the trip for months, because it was the first time all of us, including my grandparents, could take time off together. We booked a small boat tour and spent a night on the bay, which turned out to be the highlight of the whole holiday.\n\nDuring the day, we sailed between the islands, stopped to explore a huge cave full of stalactites, and went kayaking through narrow channels between the rocks. My younger brother and I also tried swimming off the side of the boat, although the water was colder than I expected. In the evening, we sat on the deck, ate fresh seafood and watched the sun go down behind the islands.\n\nThe reason it impressed me so much is the sheer scale and beauty of the landscape. Photos really don't do it justice. When you're actually there, surrounded by those towering rocks and complete silence, you feel incredibly small, in a good way. I also remember waking up early the next morning, when there was mist over the water, and it looked almost magical. At the same time, I noticed some plastic floating near the shore, which made me realise how fragile places like this are. So it left me with a sense of wonder, but also a feeling that we need to take better care of them.", vocab: [["the highlight of", "điểm nhấn, phần đáng nhớ nhất"], ["the sheer scale", "quy mô đồ sộ"], ["not do it justice", "không lột tả hết vẻ đẹp"], ["a sense of wonder", "cảm giác kinh ngạc, thán phục"]] },
  "What are the biggest environmental problems in your country?": { a: "In Vietnam, I'd say the most pressing issue is air pollution in big cities like Hanoi and Ho Chi Minh City, mainly caused by traffic, construction and industry. Another major problem is plastic waste, which ends up in rivers and eventually the ocean. And of course, as a country with a long coastline, we're particularly vulnerable to climate change. The Mekong Delta, for instance, is already suffering from rising sea levels and saltwater intrusion, which threatens farmers' livelihoods. These problems are interconnected, so I think they need to be tackled together rather than one at a time.", vocab: [["the most pressing issue", "vấn đề cấp bách nhất"], ["be vulnerable to", "dễ bị tổn thương trước"], ["saltwater intrusion", "xâm nhập mặn"], ["threaten livelihoods", "đe dọa sinh kế"]] },
  "Who should be responsible for protecting the environment: governments or individuals?": { a: "I'd argue it has to be a shared responsibility, but governments should take the lead. The truth is that individual actions, like recycling or using less plastic, are important, yet they can only achieve so much. The biggest sources of pollution are usually industries and energy systems, and only governments have the power to regulate them, for example by introducing carbon taxes or investing in public transport. At the same time, policies only work if people actually support them. So ideally, governments set the rules and provide the infrastructure, while individuals change their everyday habits.", vocab: [["a shared responsibility", "trách nhiệm chung"], ["take the lead", "đi đầu, dẫn dắt"], ["can only achieve so much", "chỉ đạt được đến một mức nhất định"]] },
  "Will renewable energy replace fossil fuels in the future?": { a: "I think it will, eventually, but probably not as quickly as many people hope. Solar and wind power have become much cheaper over the last decade, and in some countries they're already the cheapest source of new electricity. However, there are still challenges, such as storing energy for when the sun isn't shining. Many economies are also heavily dependent on coal and oil, so the transition will take time and money. My guess is that fossil fuels will gradually be phased out over the next few decades rather than disappearing overnight.", vocab: [["be heavily dependent on", "phụ thuộc nặng nề vào"], ["be phased out", "bị loại bỏ dần"], ["overnight", "chỉ sau một đêm, ngay lập tức"]] },

  /* ===== Công việc ===== */
  "What kind of job would you like to have in the future?": { a: "Ideally, I'd love to work in marketing, preferably for an international company. I've always been interested in why people buy certain things, and marketing seems like the perfect mix of creativity and analysis. I did a short internship at an agency last summer, and I really enjoyed brainstorming campaign ideas with the team.", vocab: [["the perfect mix of", "sự kết hợp hoàn hảo giữa"], ["do an internship", "đi thực tập"], ["brainstorm ideas", "động não, cùng nghĩ ý tưởng"]] },
  "Would you prefer to work from home or in an office?": { a: "I think I'd go for a mix of both. Working from home saves a lot of commuting time and lets me focus on tasks that need deep concentration. But I'd miss the social side of office life, like chatting with colleagues over lunch. Also, I find it easier to switch off at the end of the day when work and home are separate.", vocab: [["go for", "chọn"], ["commuting time", "thời gian đi lại (đi làm)"], ["switch off", "ngắt kết nối, thôi nghĩ về công việc"]] },
  "What do you usually do after work or study?": { a: "It varies, but I usually try to do something active to clear my head. Three evenings a week, I go jogging in the park near my house. On other days, I'll cook dinner, catch up on a TV series or call my parents. I try not to look at my laptop after nine, otherwise I struggle to fall asleep.", vocab: [["clear my head", "làm đầu óc thoải mái, tỉnh táo"], ["catch up on", "xem/làm bù những gì bỏ lỡ"], ["struggle to fall asleep", "khó ngủ"]] },
  "Describe a job that you would like to do in the future.": { a: "The job I'd really like to do in the future is to become a user experience designer, or UX designer. Basically, it's someone who works out how people interact with apps and websites, and then designs them so they're easy and enjoyable to use.\n\nIn terms of skills, it requires quite a broad range. First of all, you need to understand people, so research and interviewing skills are essential. You also need a good eye for visual design and the ability to use tools like Figma. On top of that, communication is crucial, because UX designers have to explain their ideas to developers, managers and clients, and sometimes defend them when people disagree.\n\nAs for how I could get this job, I've already taken the first step. Last year, I completed an online course in UX design, and I've started building a portfolio by redesigning a few apps that I personally find frustrating. My plan is to apply for an internship at a tech company after I graduate, so that I can learn from experienced designers. I'm also thinking about doing a master's degree later on, but I'd rather get some hands-on experience first.\n\nThe main reason I'd like to do this job is that it combines two things I love: creativity and problem-solving. I got interested in it when I watched my grandmother struggle to use a banking app, and I remember thinking that the design simply wasn't made with people like her in mind. I find it really meaningful that good design can make technology accessible to everyone. Plus, it's a fast-growing field with plenty of opportunities, so it seems like a smart career choice as well.", vocab: [["a good eye for", "có con mắt tinh tường về"], ["take the first step", "bước bước đầu tiên"], ["hands-on experience", "kinh nghiệm thực tế"], ["with someone in mind", "dành cho, nghĩ đến ai"]] },
  "Is a high salary more important than job satisfaction?": { a: "Personally, I'd put job satisfaction first, although I don't think salary should be dismissed. If you dread going to work every morning, no amount of money can really make up for that, and in the long run it can take a toll on your mental health. That said, a decent salary is essential, especially for people supporting a family or living in an expensive city. For a young graduate, for example, financial security might reasonably come first. So I'd say the ideal is a job that pays enough to live comfortably and still gives you a sense of purpose.", vocab: [["dread doing something", "sợ, ngán phải làm gì"], ["make up for", "bù đắp cho"], ["take a toll on", "gây tổn hại đến"], ["a sense of purpose", "cảm giác có mục đích, ý nghĩa"]] },
  "How will automation affect employment in the future?": { a: "I think automation will have a mixed effect. In the short term, workers in manufacturing, transport and administration are probably the most at risk, because machines and software can already do many of their tasks faster and more cheaply. That could lead to unemployment in certain regions. In the long run, however, history suggests that new technologies tend to create new kinds of jobs that we can't even imagine yet. The key issue is the transition period. Unless governments and companies invest seriously in retraining, a lot of people could be left behind.", vocab: [["a mixed effect", "tác động vừa tốt vừa xấu"], ["be most at risk", "chịu rủi ro cao nhất"], ["in the long run", "về lâu dài"], ["be left behind", "bị bỏ lại phía sau"]] },
  "Why do some people change careers later in life?": { a: "There are several reasons, I think. Some people simply realise that the career they chose at twenty doesn't suit who they've become. Others experience burnout after years in a stressful job and want something more balanced. A friend of my father's, for example, left banking in his forties to open a small bakery, because he wanted to do something with his hands. Changes in the economy also play a part, since some industries shrink and force people to retrain. And these days, with online courses so widely available, switching careers seems far less risky than it used to be.", vocab: [["experience burnout", "bị kiệt sức (vì công việc)"], ["play a part", "đóng một vai trò"], ["switch careers", "chuyển nghề"]] },

  /* ===== Du lịch ===== */
  "Do you like travelling?": { a: "Yes, I absolutely love it. For me, travelling is the best way to step out of my comfort zone and see how other people live. I don't need to go anywhere exotic, though. Even a weekend trip to a nearby province can recharge my batteries, especially if I get to try some local food.", vocab: [["step out of my comfort zone", "bước ra khỏi vùng an toàn"], ["recharge my batteries", "nạp lại năng lượng"], ["local food", "đồ ăn địa phương"]] },
  "Where did you go on your last holiday?": { a: "My last holiday was a four-day trip to Da Lat with a couple of university friends. It's a hill town in the Central Highlands, so it was a nice escape from the heat. We spent most of our time wandering around flower gardens, sipping coffee in little cafés and exploring the night market.", vocab: [["a nice escape from", "một sự trốn chạy dễ chịu khỏi"], ["wander around", "đi dạo loanh quanh"], ["sip coffee", "nhâm nhi cà phê"]] },
  "Do you prefer travelling alone or with friends?": { a: "I generally prefer travelling with friends, because sharing experiences makes them more memorable, and it's cheaper when you split the costs. Having said that, I went on a short solo trip last year, and I was surprised by how much I enjoyed the freedom. I could change my plans on a whim without having to consult anyone.", vocab: [["split the costs", "chia tiền, chia chi phí"], ["a solo trip", "chuyến đi một mình"], ["on a whim", "theo hứng, tùy hứng"]] },
  "Describe a memorable trip you have taken.": { a: "I'd like to talk about a trip I took to Hoi An, the ancient town in central Vietnam, about three years ago, right after I finished my final high school exams.\n\nI went with my three closest friends from school. We'd been stuck indoors revising for months, so we promised each other that as soon as the exams were over, we'd go somewhere together to celebrate. We didn't have much money, so we took an overnight train from Hanoi, which was an adventure in itself. We stayed up most of the night chatting and playing cards in our tiny sleeper cabin.\n\nOnce we arrived, we spent four days exploring the old town. We rented bicycles and rode through the narrow streets lined with yellow houses, and we spent hours trying street food, especially cao lau and banh mi. One day, we cycled out to An Bang beach, and another day we took a cooking class where we learnt to make spring rolls from scratch. On our last night, during the full-moon festival, we released paper lanterns onto the river and each made a wish.\n\nThe reason this trip was so memorable is that it marked the end of an important chapter in our lives. We knew we'd soon be going to different universities in different cities, so there was a bittersweet feeling throughout the trip. I remember sitting by the river on that last night, watching the lanterns float away, and feeling both excited about the future and a bit sad. It was also the first time I'd travelled without my parents, so I felt really independent and grown-up. Even now, whenever the four of us meet up, we always end up reminiscing about that trip.", vocab: [["an adventure in itself", "bản thân nó đã là một cuộc phiêu lưu"], ["from scratch", "từ đầu, từ nguyên liệu thô"], ["mark the end of an important chapter", "đánh dấu sự kết thúc một chương quan trọng"], ["a bittersweet feeling", "cảm giác vừa vui vừa buồn"]] },
  "What are the benefits of international tourism for a country?": { a: "The most obvious benefit is economic. Tourism brings in foreign currency and creates jobs, not just in hotels and restaurants, but also for taxi drivers, guides and local craftspeople. In Vietnam, for instance, places like Hoi An have been transformed by tourism. Beyond money, it can also boost a country's international image and encourage the government to preserve historical sites and natural landscapes. There's a cultural benefit too, because contact with visitors can make people more open-minded. Of course, all of this depends on tourism being well managed, otherwise the costs can start to outweigh the gains.", vocab: [["bring in foreign currency", "mang về ngoại tệ"], ["boost a country's image", "nâng cao hình ảnh quốc gia"], ["open-minded", "cởi mở"]] },
  "Can tourism damage local culture?": { a: "I think it certainly can, if it isn't handled carefully. When a place becomes very popular, traditions are sometimes turned into performances just for tourists, and they gradually lose their original meaning. In some villages in the mountains, for example, traditional costumes are now worn mainly for photos rather than in daily life. Rising prices can also push local people out of their own neighbourhoods. That said, tourism can also help keep traditions alive, because it gives communities a financial reason to preserve their crafts and festivals. So it really comes down to how responsibly it's managed.", vocab: [["lose their original meaning", "mất đi ý nghĩa ban đầu"], ["push someone out of", "đẩy ai ra khỏi"], ["keep traditions alive", "giữ gìn truyền thống"], ["come down to", "suy cho cùng phụ thuộc vào"]] },
  "How might travel change in the next twenty years?": { a: "I imagine travel will become more sustainable, partly out of necessity. As concerns about climate change grow, we might see more people choosing trains over short flights, and airlines may switch to cleaner fuels. Technology will probably make the whole process smoother too, with things like facial recognition replacing passports at airports. It's also possible that virtual reality will let people 'visit' places they can't afford to travel to, although I doubt it will ever replace the real thing. And with popular destinations becoming overcrowded, more governments might limit visitor numbers or introduce tourist taxes.", vocab: [["out of necessity", "vì bắt buộc, vì cần thiết"], ["choose A over B", "chọn A thay vì B"], ["the real thing", "trải nghiệm thật"], ["overcrowded", "quá đông đúc"]] },

  /* ===== Sức khỏe ===== */
  "What do you do to stay healthy?": { a: "I try to keep things simple. I go for a run three times a week and cycle to class whenever the weather allows. I also make an effort to eat plenty of vegetables and cut back on sugary drinks, which used to be my weakness. And I've realised that getting enough sleep makes a huge difference to my energy levels.", vocab: [["make an effort to", "cố gắng"], ["cut back on", "giảm bớt"], ["energy levels", "mức năng lượng"]] },
  "Do you enjoy cooking?": { a: "Yes, I've really got into it over the past year. When I first moved out of my parents' house, I lived on instant noodles, but I soon got tired of that. Now I enjoy experimenting with simple recipes, especially Vietnamese dishes my mum used to make. Cooking after a long day actually helps me unwind.", vocab: [["get into something", "bắt đầu thích, mê điều gì"], ["live on", "sống nhờ vào, ăn chủ yếu"], ["experiment with", "thử nghiệm với"]] },
  "How much sleep do you usually get?": { a: "On weekdays, I usually get around six or seven hours, which is probably a bit less than I need. I tend to stay up late finishing assignments, and then I have early classes. At weekends, I try to catch up on sleep, although I know that isn't the healthiest habit. I'm trying to go to bed earlier.", vocab: [["stay up late", "thức khuya"], ["catch up on sleep", "ngủ bù"], ["the healthiest habit", "thói quen lành mạnh nhất"]] },
  "Describe a healthy habit you would like to develop.": { a: "One healthy habit I'd really like to develop is waking up early, ideally around half past five, and using that time to exercise and plan my day.\n\nTo be honest, I haven't managed to develop this habit yet, mainly because I'm a bit of a night owl. My brain seems to work best late at night, so I often end up studying or scrolling on my phone until after midnight. Then, of course, when my alarm goes off at half five, I just hit the snooze button and go back to sleep. I've tried several times, but I usually give up after a few days, especially when the weather is cold or I've had a stressful week.\n\nAs for how I could start, or rather how I plan to start this time, I think the key is to change my evenings first rather than my mornings. I'm going to set a rule that I put my phone away at ten o'clock and leave it charging in the kitchen, so I'm not tempted to check it in bed. I'll also lay out my running clothes the night before, so there are fewer excuses in the morning. And instead of jumping straight to half five, I'll set my alarm fifteen minutes earlier each week, so the change is gradual.\n\nI'm sure this habit would improve my life in several ways. Physically, I'd get regular exercise without it eating into my day. Mentally, I'd start each morning feeling calm and in control, instead of rushing out of the door. Several of my friends who are early risers say they're far more productive, and I'd love to experience that for myself. In the long run, I think it would make me healthier, less stressed and more disciplined overall.", vocab: [["a night owl", "cú đêm, người hay thức khuya"], ["hit the snooze button", "bấm nút báo thức lại"], ["eat into", "lấn vào, chiếm mất (thời gian)"], ["an early riser", "người dậy sớm"]] },
  "Why are obesity rates increasing in many countries?": { a: "I think it's mainly a combination of diet and lifestyle. Processed food and sugary drinks have become cheap and widely available, and they're often marketed very aggressively, especially to children. At the same time, people are far less active than before. Many of us sit at a desk all day, drive or ride motorbikes everywhere, and spend our free time in front of screens. Busy schedules also play a role, because when people are short of time, they tend to rely on fast food rather than cooking. So it isn't simply a matter of individual willpower; the environment we live in matters a great deal.", vocab: [["processed food", "thực phẩm chế biến sẵn"], ["be short of time", "thiếu thời gian"], ["a matter of willpower", "vấn đề ý chí"]] },
  "Should the government tax unhealthy food?": { a: "On balance, I'm in favour of it, particularly for sugary drinks. There's evidence from countries like the UK and Mexico that taxes on soft drinks have reduced consumption and pushed companies to cut the sugar in their products. The money raised could also be spent on healthcare or sports facilities. However, I can see the argument against it. Such taxes may hit low-income families hardest, since they spend a larger share of their income on food. So I think a tax works best when it's combined with making healthy food cheaper and easier to access.", vocab: [["on balance", "cân nhắc mọi mặt"], ["be in favour of", "ủng hộ"], ["hit someone hardest", "ảnh hưởng nặng nề nhất đến ai"]] },
  "Is mental health as important as physical health?": { a: "Absolutely, and I'd even say the two are closely linked. If someone is suffering from anxiety or depression, it often affects their sleep, their appetite and their ability to work, so their physical health suffers too. The problem is that mental health issues are often invisible, and in many cultures, including mine, people still feel ashamed to talk about them. Fortunately, attitudes are slowly changing, especially among younger people. I think schools and workplaces should treat mental health just as seriously as a broken leg, by offering counselling and encouraging people to seek help early.", vocab: [["be closely linked", "liên quan chặt chẽ"], ["feel ashamed to", "cảm thấy xấu hổ khi"], ["seek help", "tìm kiếm sự giúp đỡ"]] },

  /* ===== Truyền thông ===== */
  "How do you usually get the news?": { a: "Mostly through my phone, to be honest. I follow a couple of reliable news websites, and I glance at the headlines during my morning commute. I also listen to a news podcast while I'm cooking. I rarely buy a newspaper these days, although my dad still reads one every morning with his coffee.", vocab: [["glance at the headlines", "lướt qua các tiêu đề"], ["morning commute", "quãng đường đi làm/học buổi sáng"], ["reliable news websites", "trang tin đáng tin cậy"]] },
  "Do you watch videos online every day?": { a: "Yes, pretty much every day. I usually watch a few short videos on YouTube while having lunch, often cooking tutorials or travel vlogs. I also use online videos for studying, for example, explanations of tricky grammar points. The only problem is that it's easy to lose track of time, so I try to set myself a limit.", vocab: [["pretty much", "gần như"], ["lose track of time", "quên mất thời gian"], ["set myself a limit", "tự đặt giới hạn"]] },
  "Do you trust the information you read on social media?": { a: "Not really, or at least not straight away. There's so much misleading content out there that I've learned to take everything with a pinch of salt. If I see a shocking story, I usually check whether a reputable news outlet has reported it before I believe it, and I definitely don't share it until I've double-checked.", vocab: [["misleading content", "nội dung gây hiểu lầm"], ["take something with a pinch of salt", "không hoàn toàn tin điều gì"], ["a reputable news outlet", "cơ quan báo chí uy tín"]] },
  "Describe an advertisement that you remember well.": { a: "The advertisement I remember best is a TV commercial for a brand of instant coffee that came out a few years ago, around the Lunar New Year.\n\nI first saw it on television at my grandparents' house, during the week before Tet, and then it was everywhere, on YouTube, on Facebook and even on big screens at bus stops. I think I must have watched it dozens of times without getting bored, which is quite unusual for an advert.\n\nWhat was clever about it was that it barely showed the product at all. Instead, it told a short story about a young man who had moved to the city for work and hadn't been home for several years. He was always too busy, always promising his elderly father that he'd visit next time. In the advert, he finally takes the long bus journey back to his village on New Year's Eve, and his father, who had been waiting by the gate, makes him a cup of coffee. There was hardly any dialogue, just gentle music and the sound of rain. Only at the very end did the brand's logo appear, with a short line about coming home.\n\nThe main reason I remember it is that it struck a chord with me personally. At the time, I'd just started university in another city, and I was feeling quite homesick. I remember my eyes welling up the first time I saw it, and I immediately called my parents afterwards. I think it was a brilliant example of emotional advertising, because instead of telling people to buy coffee, it connected the brand with family and nostalgia. To be honest, I still associate that brand with going home for Tet.", vocab: [["strike a chord with", "chạm đến cảm xúc của ai"], ["feel homesick", "nhớ nhà"], ["eyes well up", "rưng rưng nước mắt"], ["associate A with B", "liên tưởng A với B"]] },
  "Should advertising aimed at children be restricted?": { a: "Yes, I strongly believe it should be. Young children aren't able to tell the difference between entertainment and a sales pitch, so they're easily persuaded by colourful characters and catchy songs. This is especially worrying when it comes to junk food and toys, because children then pester their parents for things they don't need. Some countries, like Sweden, have already banned TV adverts aimed at under-twelves. Of course, it's much harder to regulate online, since adverts are often disguised as videos by influencers. But I think stricter rules, along with teaching children media literacy, would make a real difference.", vocab: [["a sales pitch", "lời chào hàng"], ["pester someone for something", "nằng nặc đòi ai thứ gì"], ["be disguised as", "được ngụy trang thành"], ["media literacy", "hiểu biết về truyền thông"]] },
  "How can people recognise fake news?": { a: "I think the first step is simply to slow down. Fake news is often designed to provoke a strong emotional reaction, so if a headline makes you angry or shocked, that's a warning sign. People should also check the source: is it a well-known news outlet, or a website they've never heard of? Another useful habit is cross-checking, which means seeing whether other reliable sources are reporting the same story. With images, a reverse image search can reveal whether a photo is old or taken out of context. Ultimately, though, I believe schools need to teach these skills from an early age.", vocab: [["provoke a strong reaction", "kích động phản ứng mạnh"], ["a warning sign", "dấu hiệu cảnh báo"], ["taken out of context", "bị tách khỏi ngữ cảnh"]] },
  "Will newspapers disappear in the future?": { a: "Printed newspapers will probably become much rarer, but I don't think they'll vanish completely. Most young people already get their news online, and printing and distributing paper is expensive, so many publications have cut back on their print editions or gone fully digital. However, there will likely remain a niche market of older readers and people who simply enjoy the ritual of reading a paper over breakfast. More importantly, I think newspapers as organisations will survive, because we'll always need professional journalists. They'll just reach us through apps and websites rather than on paper.", vocab: [["go fully digital", "chuyển hoàn toàn sang kỹ thuật số"], ["a niche market", "thị trường ngách"], ["the ritual of", "thói quen, nghi thức (hằng ngày) của việc"]] },

  /* ===== Thành phố ===== */
  "Where is your hometown?": { a: "My hometown is Nam Dinh, a small city about ninety kilometres south of Hanoi. It's not a famous tourist destination, but it's known for its textile industry and for a delicious type of noodle soup. It's quite peaceful compared with Hanoi, and life there moves at a much gentler pace.", vocab: [["be known for", "nổi tiếng về"], ["a tourist destination", "điểm đến du lịch"], ["at a gentler pace", "với nhịp độ nhẹ nhàng hơn"]] },
  "What do you like most about the place where you live?": { a: "What I like most is the sense of community. I live in a small alley in Hanoi, and the neighbours all know each other. In the evenings, people sit outside chatting, and the older ladies always keep an eye on the children playing. It feels much friendlier than living in a big apartment block where nobody says hello.", vocab: [["a sense of community", "tinh thần cộng đồng"], ["keep an eye on", "để mắt trông chừng"], ["an apartment block", "khu chung cư"]] },
  "How do you usually travel around your city?": { a: "Like most people in Vietnam, I mainly get around by motorbike, because it's fast and convenient for weaving through traffic. For longer journeys across the city, I sometimes use ride-hailing apps. Recently, I've started taking the new metro line now and then, which is surprisingly comfortable, and it means I can read on the way.", vocab: [["get around", "đi lại"], ["weave through traffic", "luồn lách qua dòng xe"], ["ride-hailing apps", "ứng dụng gọi xe"]] },
  "Describe a city you would like to live in.": { a: "The city I'd love to live in one day is Melbourne, in the south-east of Australia.\n\nI first got to know about it through my older cousin, who moved there to do a master's degree about five years ago and has stayed on to work. She's always sending me photos and telling me stories about her life there, and I've also watched quite a few travel vlogs and documentaries about it. Melbourne is regularly ranked as one of the world's most liveable cities, so I suppose that caught my attention as well.\n\nFrom what I've seen and heard, it's a really vibrant and multicultural city. It's famous for its coffee culture, with tiny cafés tucked away in narrow lanes covered in street art. There are also lots of green spaces, like the Royal Botanic Gardens, and the beach isn't far from the city centre. Public transport seems excellent too, especially the trams, which are free in the central area. And from what my cousin says, there's always something going on, whether it's a food festival, a live music event or a big sporting match.\n\nThe main reason I'd like to live there is that it seems to offer a great balance between work and lifestyle. As someone who wants to work in marketing, I'd have plenty of career opportunities, but I'd also be able to enjoy nature and a relaxed pace of life at weekends. I really like the idea of a city where people from so many different backgrounds live side by side, because I think it would broaden my horizons. Of course, I know it's expensive and the weather can be unpredictable, but I'd definitely like to give it a try for a few years.", vocab: [["most liveable cities", "những thành phố đáng sống nhất"], ["be tucked away", "nằm khuất, ẩn mình"], ["live side by side", "sống cạnh nhau"], ["broaden my horizons", "mở rộng tầm nhìn"]] },
  "What are the main problems of living in big cities?": { a: "I'd say the most obvious problems are traffic congestion and pollution. In cities like Hanoi, people can spend hours every day stuck in traffic, breathing in exhaust fumes. Another major issue is the cost of living, especially housing, which forces many young people to live in cramped rooms far from their workplace. Big cities can also be surprisingly lonely, since people are often too busy to get to know their neighbours. Having said that, these problems exist precisely because cities offer so many opportunities, so it's really about managing growth better rather than avoiding cities altogether.", vocab: [["traffic congestion", "ùn tắc giao thông"], ["exhaust fumes", "khói xe"], ["the cost of living", "chi phí sinh hoạt"], ["cramped rooms", "phòng chật chội"]] },
  "How can governments reduce traffic congestion?": { a: "I think the most effective long-term solution is to invest heavily in public transport, so it becomes a genuinely attractive alternative to private vehicles. Metro systems and dedicated bus lanes, for example, can move huge numbers of people quickly. At the same time, governments could discourage driving in city centres, perhaps through congestion charges, as London has done. Better urban planning also matters. If schools, offices and shops are spread more evenly, people won't all need to travel to the same area at rush hour. None of these measures is a quick fix, but together they could make a real difference.", vocab: [["a genuinely attractive alternative", "một lựa chọn thay thế thật sự hấp dẫn"], ["congestion charges", "phí chống ùn tắc"], ["rush hour", "giờ cao điểm"], ["a quick fix", "giải pháp nhanh, tạm thời"]] },
  "Why do young people move from the countryside to cities?": { a: "The main reason is undoubtedly opportunity. Most universities, well-paid jobs and big companies are concentrated in cities, so young people often feel they have no choice but to move if they want to get ahead. Cities also offer a more exciting lifestyle, with better entertainment, shopping and nightlife. In my own village, for example, nearly all of my cousins have moved to Hanoi or Ho Chi Minh City for work. However, I think this trend could slow down a little in the future, as remote working becomes more common and rural areas get better infrastructure.", vocab: [["get ahead", "tiến lên, thành công"], ["have no choice but to", "không còn lựa chọn nào khác ngoài"], ["remote working", "làm việc từ xa"]] },

  /* ===== Quê hương & Nơi ở ===== */
  "Can you tell me a little about the place where you grew up?": { a: "I grew up in a small town in the Red River Delta, surrounded by rice fields. It was the kind of place where everyone knew everyone else, and kids could play outside safely until dinner time. There wasn't much to do in terms of entertainment, but looking back, I had a really carefree childhood there.", vocab: [["everyone knew everyone else", "ai cũng biết nhau"], ["in terms of", "về mặt"], ["a carefree childhood", "tuổi thơ vô tư"]] },
  "Do you live in a house or an apartment at the moment?": { a: "At the moment, I'm renting a small apartment with a friend, about fifteen minutes from my university. It's on the eighth floor, so we get a decent view of the city and plenty of natural light. It's a bit cramped, to be honest, but it's affordable and close to everything I need.", vocab: [["a decent view", "tầm nhìn khá đẹp"], ["natural light", "ánh sáng tự nhiên"], ["affordable", "giá cả phải chăng"]] },
  "Which room in your home do you spend the most time in?": { a: "Definitely my bedroom, because it doubles as my study. I've got a desk by the window where I do all my coursework, and it's also where I relax in the evenings. I've tried to make it cosy with some plants and fairy lights, so it feels like my own little retreat from the busy city outside.", vocab: [["double as", "kiêm luôn chức năng"], ["cosy", "ấm cúng"], ["a retreat from", "nơi ẩn náu, chốn nghỉ ngơi khỏi"]] },
  "Would you like to move somewhere else in the future?": { a: "Yes, I think so, although not permanently. I'd love to spend a few years living abroad, perhaps in Australia or Canada, to gain some international work experience and improve my English. But in the long term, I can see myself settling down back in Vietnam, somewhere not too far from my parents.", vocab: [["gain experience", "tích lũy kinh nghiệm"], ["in the long term", "về lâu dài"], ["settle down", "ổn định cuộc sống"]] },
  "Describe a home you would like to live in one day.": { a: "The home I'd love to live in one day is a small wooden house in the hills just outside Da Lat, in the Central Highlands of Vietnam.\n\nIdeally, it would be on a gentle slope with a view over a valley of pine trees, but still within twenty minutes' drive of the town, so I wouldn't feel completely cut off. Da Lat has a cool climate all year round, which is a big part of the appeal for someone like me, who really struggles with the heat in Hanoi.\n\nIn terms of what it would look like, I picture something simple but full of character. It would have large windows to let in plenty of natural light, a wooden veranda where I could drink my morning coffee, and a fireplace for the chilly evenings. Inside, I'd want an open-plan kitchen and living room, plus a small study lined with bookshelves. Outside, I'd love to have a vegetable garden, because I've always dreamt of growing my own food.\n\nAs for who I'd live there with, I imagine sharing it with my future partner and maybe a dog or two. I'd also like to have a spare room, so my parents could come and stay for long periods, especially when they retire.\n\nThe reason this kind of home appeals to me is that it represents a slower, more balanced way of life. At the moment, my life is quite hectic, and I spend most of my time in a cramped apartment surrounded by traffic. A house like this would give me space to breathe, both literally and mentally. I know it's a bit of a dream, and I'd need to find a job that lets me work remotely, but it's something I'm genuinely working towards.", vocab: [["cut off", "bị cô lập, tách biệt"], ["full of character", "có nét riêng, đầy cá tính"], ["hectic", "bận rộn, hối hả"], ["work towards", "nỗ lực hướng tới"]] },
  "Why do many young people find it hard to buy their own home nowadays?": { a: "The main reason is that property prices have risen much faster than salaries, particularly in big cities. In Hanoi, for example, a young office worker would probably need to save their entire income for decades to afford a modest apartment. On top of that, getting a mortgage often requires a large deposit, which many graduates simply don't have. Some would also argue that property is increasingly bought as an investment rather than a place to live, which pushes prices up even further. As a result, many young people end up renting for much longer than their parents did, or relying on family help.", vocab: [["property prices", "giá bất động sản"], ["a modest apartment", "một căn hộ khiêm tốn"], ["get a mortgage", "vay thế chấp mua nhà"], ["push prices up", "đẩy giá lên"]] },
  "How has the design of homes in your country changed over the last few decades?": { a: "Quite significantly, I'd say. A few decades ago, most families in Vietnam lived in low-rise houses, often with a courtyard, and several generations shared the same space. Nowadays, especially in cities, you see a lot of narrow 'tube houses' that are tall and thin, as well as high-rise apartment blocks. Modern homes also tend to be designed for smaller families, with separate bedrooms for privacy. One downside, in my opinion, is that newer buildings sometimes ignore the climate, relying on air conditioning instead of natural ventilation, which traditional houses handled very cleverly.", vocab: [["low-rise / high-rise", "thấp tầng / cao tầng"], ["several generations", "nhiều thế hệ"], ["natural ventilation", "thông gió tự nhiên"]] },
  "Is it better for people to rent or to own the place they live in?": { a: "I don't think there's a single right answer, because it depends on someone's stage of life. Owning a home offers security, and in the long run, it can be a good investment, which is why it's such a strong goal in Vietnamese culture. However, renting gives people flexibility. A young graduate who might change jobs or cities, for instance, would probably be better off renting rather than tying themselves to a huge loan. Personally, I'd rent for the first few years of my career and think about buying once I've settled down and have a stable income.", vocab: [["stage of life", "giai đoạn cuộc đời"], ["be better off doing something", "tốt hơn nên làm gì"], ["tie oneself to", "tự ràng buộc mình với"], ["a stable income", "thu nhập ổn định"]] },
  "What can local governments do to make neighbourhoods more pleasant to live in?": { a: "I think one of the most effective things is to create more green spaces, such as small parks and tree-lined streets, because they improve air quality and give people somewhere to relax. Local authorities could also make neighbourhoods more walkable by building proper pavements and keeping them clear of parked motorbikes, which is a real problem in Vietnam. Community facilities matter too, like libraries, playgrounds and sports courts, since they encourage neighbours to interact. Finally, better rubbish collection and street lighting might sound basic, but they can make a huge difference to people's quality of life and sense of safety.", vocab: [["green spaces", "không gian xanh"], ["walkable", "thuận tiện cho đi bộ"], ["quality of life", "chất lượng cuộc sống"]] },

  /* ===== Nghề nghiệp & Nơi làm việc ===== */
  "What does a typical working or study day look like for you?": { a: "On a typical weekday, I get up at around half six and have a quick breakfast before heading to university. My morning is usually taken up with lectures, and in the afternoon I either work on group projects or study in the library. In the evening, I go for a run, have dinner and then do a bit of revision.", vocab: [["head to", "đi đến"], ["be taken up with", "bị chiếm hết bởi"], ["do a bit of revision", "ôn bài một chút"]] },
  "Do you get on well with the people you work or study with?": { a: "Yes, for the most part. My classmates are friendly and supportive, and we often help each other out with assignments. Of course, there are occasional disagreements during group projects, especially when deadlines are tight, but we usually manage to sort things out by talking openly. A few of them have actually become close friends.", vocab: [["get on well with", "hòa thuận với"], ["help each other out", "giúp đỡ lẫn nhau"], ["sort things out", "giải quyết ổn thỏa"]] },
  "What is the most enjoyable part of your job or course?": { a: "I'd say the most enjoyable part is working on real case studies. In some modules, local companies give us actual business problems to solve, and we present our ideas to their managers. It's quite nerve-racking, but it's really rewarding when they take our suggestions seriously. It makes the theory feel much more relevant.", vocab: [["case studies", "tình huống nghiên cứu thực tế"], ["nerve-racking", "căng thẳng, hồi hộp"], ["rewarding", "đáng công sức, bổ ích"]] },
  "Have you ever had a part-time job?": { a: "Yes, I worked as a barista in a coffee shop for about a year during my first year at university. The pay wasn't great, but I learnt a lot about dealing with customers and staying calm under pressure, especially during the morning rush. It also taught me to manage my time, because I had to balance shifts with my studies.", vocab: [["deal with customers", "tiếp xúc, xử lý khách hàng"], ["stay calm under pressure", "giữ bình tĩnh dưới áp lực"], ["balance A with B", "cân bằng A với B"]] },
  "Describe a person you know who is very good at their job.": { a: "I'd like to talk about my aunt, Lan, who is a paediatric nurse at a large children's hospital in Hanoi. She's my mother's younger sister, and she's been working there for nearly twenty years.\n\nI know her very well because she lived with my family for a few years when I was a child, and even now we see each other almost every weekend. I've also seen her at work a couple of times, once when my younger brother was admitted to hospital with a high fever, and that's when I really understood how good she is at what she does.\n\nNursing children is an incredibly demanding job. She often works long night shifts, and she has to deal not only with sick children but also with parents who are frightened and sometimes angry. Despite that, she always seems calm and patient. When my brother was in hospital, he was terrified of needles, but she somehow managed to distract him with jokes and stories, and he hardly noticed the injection.\n\nI think several things make her so successful at her work. First of all, she has a real gift for putting people at ease. She talks to children at their level and explains things to parents in plain language, without any medical jargon. Secondly, she's extremely well organised and pays close attention to detail, which is vital in a job where small mistakes can have serious consequences. Most importantly, though, she genuinely cares. She's told me that even after twenty years, she still feels a sense of satisfaction every time a child goes home healthy. Her colleagues clearly respect her a lot, and she's often asked to train new nurses. Honestly, she's one of the people I admire most.", vocab: [["a demanding job", "công việc đòi hỏi cao"], ["have a gift for", "có năng khiếu về"], ["put people at ease", "làm người khác thấy thoải mái"], ["pay close attention to detail", "chú ý kỹ đến từng chi tiết"]] },
  "Is job satisfaction more important than a high salary?": { a: "In my view, job satisfaction matters more in the long term, but it would be naive to say money isn't important. If you're constantly unhappy at work, it tends to spill over into the rest of your life, affecting your health and relationships. On the other hand, a job you love won't feel very satisfying if you can't pay your rent. I think the answer also shifts over time. When people are starting out, they might reasonably prioritise earning and saving, whereas later they may be willing to accept a pay cut for more meaningful work.", vocab: [["it would be naive to", "thật ngây thơ khi"], ["spill over into", "lan sang, ảnh hưởng tới"], ["accept a pay cut", "chấp nhận giảm lương"]] },
  "How might artificial intelligence change the kinds of jobs people do in the future?": { a: "I think AI will gradually shift human work towards tasks that machines find difficult, such as creative thinking, complex decision-making and anything involving empathy. Routine jobs, like processing documents or answering basic customer questions, are likely to be largely automated. But at the same time, completely new roles will emerge, for example people who design, supervise or check AI systems. I also suspect that many existing professions will simply evolve. A teacher, for instance, might use AI to personalise lessons and spend more time mentoring students. So adaptability will probably be the most valuable skill of all.", vocab: [["shift towards", "chuyển dịch sang"], ["be largely automated", "phần lớn được tự động hóa"], ["adaptability", "khả năng thích nghi"]] },
  "Why do some people stay with the same company for their whole career?": { a: "There are a few reasons, I think. For some people, it's about security. A stable job with a pension and a predictable income can be very reassuring, especially if they have a family to support. Others genuinely feel a strong sense of loyalty, perhaps because the company has treated them well or given them opportunities to grow. My grandfather, for example, worked for the same state-owned factory for over thirty years and was proud of it. That said, this is becoming much less common among younger generations, who tend to change jobs more often to gain new skills or higher pay.", vocab: [["a predictable income", "thu nhập ổn định, dự đoán được"], ["a sense of loyalty", "lòng trung thành"], ["opportunities to grow", "cơ hội phát triển"]] },
  "Should employers be responsible for the mental health of their staff?": { a: "I believe they should take at least some responsibility, since work is one of the biggest sources of stress in people's lives. Excessive workloads, long hours and toxic management can seriously damage employees' mental health. So companies have a duty to create a healthy working environment, for instance by setting reasonable deadlines and offering access to counselling. It's also in their own interest, because burnt-out staff are less productive and more likely to quit. However, I wouldn't say employers are entirely responsible. Mental health is affected by many factors outside work, so it's really a shared responsibility.", vocab: [["excessive workloads", "khối lượng công việc quá tải"], ["a toxic working environment / toxic management", "môi trường/quản lý độc hại"], ["in their own interest", "vì lợi ích của chính họ"], ["burnt-out", "kiệt sức"]] },

  /* ===== Thời gian rảnh & Sở thích ===== */
  "What do you usually do when you have some free time?": { a: "It depends on how much time I have. If it's just an hour or so, I'll usually read or listen to a podcast. At weekends, I like to get out of the house, so I'll meet friends for coffee or go cycling around West Lake. Every now and then, I also spend a whole afternoon just baking.", vocab: [["get out of the house", "ra khỏi nhà"], ["every now and then", "thỉnh thoảng"], ["or so", "khoảng chừng"]] },
  "Have your hobbies changed since you were a child?": { a: "Yes, quite a lot. As a child, I was obsessed with drawing and collecting stickers, and I spent hours flying kites with my cousins. These days, my hobbies are more about relaxing after a busy week, like reading or going to the gym. Sadly, I've completely lost the habit of drawing, though I'd like to pick it up again.", vocab: [["be obsessed with", "bị ám ảnh, mê mẩn"], ["lose the habit of", "mất thói quen"], ["pick something up again", "bắt đầu lại điều gì"]] },
  "Do you prefer relaxing at home or going out at weekends?": { a: "I'm a bit of a homebody, so I'd usually choose a quiet weekend at home, especially after a hectic week. I love having a lie-in, cooking something nice and watching a film. That said, if I stay in for the whole weekend, I start to get restless, so I usually go out at least once to see friends.", vocab: [["a homebody", "người thích ở nhà"], ["have a lie-in", "ngủ nướng"], ["get restless", "bồn chồn, không yên"]] },
  "Is there a new hobby you would like to try?": { a: "Yes, I've been meaning to try rock climbing for ages. A couple of my friends go to an indoor climbing centre every week, and from what they say, it's both physically challenging and a great way to clear your mind. I'm a little afraid of heights, to be honest, so it would also be a good way to face that fear.", vocab: [["have been meaning to", "định làm từ lâu"], ["for ages", "từ rất lâu"], ["face a fear", "đối mặt với nỗi sợ"]] },
  "Describe a hobby that you started recently.": { a: "A hobby I took up quite recently is film photography, which means taking photos with an old-fashioned camera that uses rolls of film instead of a memory card.\n\nI started it about eight months ago, almost by accident. I was helping my grandparents clear out their storeroom, and I came across my grandfather's old camera in a dusty box. It was a Russian model from the 1980s, and he told me he'd used it to take most of our family photos when my mum was a child. He offered to let me keep it, and I thought, why not give it a go? I watched a few online tutorials, bought some film from a little shop in the Old Quarter, and that was it, really.\n\nThese days, I go out shooting maybe once or twice a month, usually on a Sunday morning when the light is soft and the streets are quiet. I don't take that many photos each time, because each roll has only thirty-six shots and getting it developed isn't cheap. So I have to think carefully about every single picture.\n\nI think that's exactly why I enjoy it so much. With my phone, I just take hundreds of photos without thinking, and I rarely look at them again. Film photography, on the other hand, forces me to slow down and really pay attention to what's around me, like the way the light falls on an old building or the expression on a street vendor's face. There's also something exciting about waiting a week to see how the photos turn out. Some are disappointing, of course, but when one turns out well, it's incredibly satisfying. And it makes me feel more connected to my grandfather, which is a lovely bonus.", vocab: [["take up a hobby", "bắt đầu một sở thích"], ["come across", "tình cờ tìm thấy"], ["give it a go", "thử xem sao"], ["turn out", "hóa ra, có kết quả"]] },
  "Why do some people find it difficult to make time for hobbies?": { a: "I think the main reason is that modern life is incredibly busy. Many people work long hours, commute for an hour or more each day and then have family responsibilities when they get home, so by the evening they're simply too exhausted to do anything active. There's also a cultural factor. In some societies, including mine, hobbies are sometimes seen as a waste of time compared with work or study. And honestly, smartphones play a role too, because scrolling is so easy that it quietly eats up the free time we do have.", vocab: [["make time for", "dành thời gian cho"], ["family responsibilities", "trách nhiệm gia đình"], ["a waste of time", "sự lãng phí thời gian"], ["eat up", "ngốn mất"]] },
  "Do you think hobbies should be useful, or is enjoyment enough?": { a: "Personally, I think enjoyment is more than enough. The whole point of a hobby is to give us a break from the pressure to be productive, so if every hobby had to be useful, it would just feel like another job. Activities like painting or gardening are valuable precisely because they help people relax and recharge. That said, many hobbies turn out to be useful anyway. Playing a sport keeps you fit, and learning a language might help your career. But I'd say those benefits should be a bonus, not the main reason for doing it.", vocab: [["the whole point of", "toàn bộ mục đích của"], ["give someone a break from", "giúp ai tạm thoát khỏi"], ["a bonus", "lợi ích thêm"]] },
  "How do the leisure activities of older and younger generations differ?": { a: "In my country, the difference is quite striking. Older people tend to prefer activities that are social and outdoors, such as doing group exercise in the park early in the morning, playing chess or meeting friends over tea. Younger people, on the other hand, spend a lot more of their free time on screens, whether that's gaming, streaming or social media. Having said that, the line is becoming blurred. Plenty of young people are into hiking or pickleball, and many grandparents now watch videos on their phones. So it's more a difference of degree than a complete divide.", vocab: [["quite striking", "khá rõ rệt"], ["be into something", "thích, mê điều gì"], ["the line is becoming blurred", "ranh giới đang dần mờ đi"], ["a difference of degree", "khác biệt về mức độ"]] },
  "Can a hobby ever turn into a successful career?": { a: "Yes, definitely, and it's becoming more common thanks to the internet. Social media has made it possible for people to share their passion with a huge audience, so a cooking hobby might become a popular YouTube channel or a small online business. I know someone who started making handmade jewellery for fun and now sells it full-time. However, I think there's a risk too. Once a hobby becomes your livelihood, you have to deal with deadlines, customers and financial pressure, and the joy can sometimes fade. So it can work, but it requires business skills, not just passion.", vocab: [["share their passion with", "chia sẻ đam mê với"], ["livelihood", "kế sinh nhai"], ["the joy can fade", "niềm vui có thể phai nhạt"]] },

  /* ===== Âm nhạc ===== */
  "What kind of music do you listen to most often?": { a: "I mostly listen to indie pop and acoustic music, both Vietnamese and international. I like songs with meaningful lyrics and a relaxed vibe, rather than anything too loud. Recently, I've also been getting into jazz, because a friend introduced me to some classic albums, and I find it great background music for studying.", vocab: [["a relaxed vibe", "không khí thư thái"], ["get into", "bắt đầu thích"], ["background music", "nhạc nền"]] },
  "Do you usually listen to music while doing other things?": { a: "Yes, almost all the time. I listen to music while I'm commuting, cooking or doing housework, because it makes boring tasks go by more quickly. When I'm studying, though, I have to be careful. Songs with lyrics distract me, so I stick to instrumental music or lo-fi playlists, which help me stay focused.", vocab: [["go by more quickly", "trôi qua nhanh hơn"], ["stick to", "chỉ dùng, trung thành với"], ["stay focused", "giữ tập trung"]] },
  "Did you learn to play a musical instrument as a child?": { a: "Yes, my parents sent me to piano lessons when I was about seven. I have to admit that I didn't enjoy practising very much, and I gave up after about four years. Looking back, I really regret quitting, because I've completely forgotten how to read music. I'd love to take it up again one day.", vocab: [["I have to admit", "phải thừa nhận rằng"], ["give up", "bỏ cuộc"], ["read music", "đọc bản nhạc"]] },
  "Have you ever been to a live concert?": { a: "Yes, a few times. The most memorable one was an outdoor concert by a Vietnamese indie band in Hanoi last year. The atmosphere was electric, and thousands of people were singing along to every song. It was so much more powerful than listening through headphones, and I lost my voice the next day from all the singing.", vocab: [["the atmosphere was electric", "bầu không khí cực kỳ sôi động"], ["sing along to", "hát theo"], ["lose my voice", "mất giọng"]] },
  "Describe a song or piece of music that has special meaning for you.": { a: "The piece of music I'd like to talk about is a Vietnamese song called 'Mẹ yêu', which is a gentle ballad about a mother's love.\n\nI first heard it when I was about eight years old. My mum used to play it on an old cassette player while she was cooking dinner, and she would often hum along. I didn't pay much attention to the lyrics at the time; I just liked the melody, which is soft and quite slow. It was simply part of the background of my childhood, along with the smell of her cooking.\n\nThese days, I don't listen to it every day, but I'd say I put it on maybe once or twice a month, usually late at night when I'm feeling a bit low or missing home. I've saved it at the top of one of my playlists, so it's always easy to find. And every year on my mum's birthday, I send her a short video of myself singing a few lines of it, which has become a bit of a family joke because my singing is terrible.\n\nThe reason it means so much to me is that it reminds me of my mother and of home. When I moved to Hanoi for university, I really struggled with homesickness during the first few months. One evening, I happened to hear this song in a café, and it brought back so many memories that I almost burst into tears. Since then, it's become a kind of comfort for me. Listening to it makes me feel close to my family, even when I'm far away. It's also made me appreciate the lyrics properly for the first time, and I've realised how much my mum sacrificed for us.", vocab: [["hum along", "ngân nga theo"], ["feel a bit low", "cảm thấy hơi buồn"], ["bring back memories", "gợi lại kỷ niệm"], ["burst into tears", "bật khóc"]] },
  "How has streaming changed the way people discover new music?": { a: "Enormously, I'd say. In the past, people mainly discovered music through the radio, TV or recommendations from friends, and you had to buy a whole album to hear it. Now, streaming platforms use algorithms to suggest songs based on what you already like, so new music is constantly being pushed towards us. That's great for exposure, and it's helped lots of independent artists find an audience without a record label. On the downside, I think algorithms can trap people in a bubble, because they keep recommending similar songs. So in a way, there's more choice but sometimes less variety.", vocab: [["find an audience", "tìm được khán giả"], ["a record label", "hãng thu âm"], ["trap people in a bubble", "nhốt người dùng trong một vòng lặp, bong bóng"]] },
  "Should music be a compulsory subject in schools?": { a: "I think it should be, at least in primary school and the early years of secondary school. Music isn't just about entertainment; research suggests it can improve children's memory, concentration and even their maths skills. It's also a great way for children to express themselves and work together, for example in a choir or school band. That said, I wouldn't force older teenagers to keep studying it if they have no interest, especially when they're preparing for important exams. Perhaps the best approach is to make it compulsory early on and optional later.", vocab: [["a compulsory subject", "môn học bắt buộc"], ["express themselves", "thể hiện bản thân"], ["early on", "từ sớm"]] },
  "Why do you think traditional music is less popular among young people?": { a: "I think a major reason is exposure. Young people today are surrounded by global pop music on social media and streaming platforms, whereas traditional music is rarely played in those spaces. Traditional genres, like Vietnamese 'ca trù', can also feel slow and hard to relate to, since the lyrics often use old-fashioned language. That said, I've noticed an interesting trend recently. Some young artists are blending traditional instruments with modern beats, and these songs have become surprisingly popular. So I think traditional music can win young audiences back if it's presented in a fresh way.", vocab: [["be surrounded by", "bị bao quanh bởi"], ["hard to relate to", "khó đồng cảm, khó liên hệ"], ["blend A with B", "pha trộn A với B"], ["win someone back", "giành lại ai"]] },
  "Is it fair that some musicians earn so much more money than others?": { a: "It's a difficult question. On the one hand, top stars attract huge audiences and generate a lot of income for many people, so it's understandable that they're paid accordingly. On the other hand, I don't think the gap always reflects talent or hard work. Many brilliant musicians struggle to make a living, partly because streaming platforms pay artists only a tiny amount per play. So I'd say the system isn't entirely fair. It might be more balanced if streaming companies shared their profits more evenly, giving smaller artists a better chance of surviving.", vocab: [["be paid accordingly", "được trả tương xứng"], ["make a living", "kiếm sống"], ["share profits more evenly", "chia lợi nhuận đồng đều hơn"]] },

  /* ===== Thể thao ===== */
  "Do you play any sports regularly?": { a: "Yes, I play badminton with a group of friends twice a week, usually on Tuesday and Friday evenings. It's a great way to stay in shape, and it's quite social as well, because we always grab something to eat afterwards. I'm not particularly talented, but I've improved a lot since I started last year.", vocab: [["stay in shape", "giữ dáng, giữ sức khỏe"], ["grab something to eat", "đi ăn chút gì đó"], ["not particularly talented", "không quá giỏi"]] },
  "Which sport is the most popular in your country?": { a: "Without question, it's football. Vietnamese people are absolutely crazy about it. Whenever the national team plays in a big tournament, the streets are packed with fans waving flags and honking their horns after a win. Lately, though, badminton and pickleball have become hugely popular too, especially among office workers.", vocab: [["without question", "không còn nghi ngờ gì"], ["be crazy about", "phát cuồng vì"], ["hugely popular", "cực kỳ phổ biến"]] },
  "Do you prefer watching sports on TV or in person?": { a: "I'd say in person, if I have the chance. Being in a stadium surrounded by thousands of cheering fans gives you a buzz that you just can't get from a screen. Realistically, though, I watch most matches on TV, because tickets can be expensive and it's more convenient. Plus, you get replays and expert commentary at home.", vocab: [["in person", "trực tiếp"], ["give you a buzz", "mang lại cảm giác phấn khích"], ["expert commentary", "bình luận chuyên môn"]] },
  "Did you take part in sports at school?": { a: "Yes, I was in my secondary school's volleyball team for about three years. We trained after class twice a week and competed against other schools in the district. We weren't very successful, to be honest, but I loved the team spirit, and it taught me how to cope with losing gracefully.", vocab: [["take part in", "tham gia"], ["team spirit", "tinh thần đồng đội"], ["lose gracefully", "thua một cách lịch thiệp"]] },
  "Describe a sporting event you watched or took part in.": { a: "I'd like to describe a sporting event I took part in rather than just watched, which was a ten-kilometre charity run in Hanoi about a year and a half ago.\n\nIt took place on a Sunday morning in early November, starting and finishing near Hoan Kiem Lake, right in the heart of the city. The route went around the lake and through some of the streets of the Old Quarter, which had been closed to traffic for the day. The money raised from entry fees went to a programme that builds schools in remote mountain areas, which was one of the reasons I signed up.\n\nI took part with three colleagues from my part-time job. None of us were serious runners, so we spent about two months training together in the evenings, gradually building up our distance. On the day itself, we met at half past five in the morning, when it was still dark, and the atmosphere was incredible. There were thousands of runners, music was blasting from speakers, and lots of people had come out to cheer us on.\n\nAs for how I felt, it was a real mix of emotions. At the start, I was nervous and worried that I wouldn't finish. Around the seventh kilometre, my legs felt like lead and I seriously considered walking, but one of my colleagues encouraged me to keep going. Then, when I saw the finish line, I got a sudden burst of energy and sprinted the last hundred metres. Crossing that line gave me an enormous sense of achievement, and I felt really proud of myself and of my friends. I also felt good knowing we'd contributed to a worthwhile cause. Since then, I've done two more races.", vocab: [["sign up", "đăng ký"], ["cheer someone on", "cổ vũ ai"], ["my legs felt like lead", "chân nặng như chì"], ["a sense of achievement", "cảm giác thành tựu"], ["a worthwhile cause", "mục đích ý nghĩa"]] },
  "Why do some countries invest so much money in hosting international sports events?": { a: "I think the main motivation is prestige. Hosting something like the Olympics or the World Cup puts a country in the global spotlight, so it's a chance to show off its culture, organisation and economic progress. Governments also hope for economic benefits, such as a boom in tourism and investment in infrastructure like new roads and stadiums. However, the evidence is mixed. Several host cities have been left with huge debts and empty venues that are hardly ever used afterwards. So while it can boost national pride, I think countries need to be realistic about the costs.", vocab: [["be in the global spotlight", "thu hút sự chú ý toàn cầu"], ["show off", "phô diễn, khoe"], ["the evidence is mixed", "bằng chứng chưa thống nhất"], ["national pride", "niềm tự hào dân tộc"]] },
  "Are professional athletes good role models for children?": { a: "Many of them are, I think. Top athletes show children the value of discipline, hard work and resilience, because nobody reaches that level without years of training and setbacks. Footballers who do charity work or speak out about important issues can have a very positive influence. That said, not every athlete behaves well. Some are involved in scandals, cheating or aggressive behaviour, and children might copy that. So I'd say athletes can be excellent role models, but parents and teachers should help children understand that admiring someone's talent doesn't mean copying everything they do.", vocab: [["role models", "hình mẫu, tấm gương"], ["resilience", "khả năng phục hồi, kiên cường"], ["setbacks", "thất bại, trở ngại"], ["speak out about", "lên tiếng về"]] },
  "How can governments encourage people to be more physically active?": { a: "I think the most effective approach is to make exercise part of everyday life rather than something extra. Governments could build safe cycle lanes and pleasant pavements, so people can walk or cycle to work instead of driving. Investing in free public facilities, such as parks with outdoor gym equipment, would also help, especially for people who can't afford gym memberships. In Hanoi, for example, those simple exercise machines around the lakes are always busy early in the morning. Schools also have a big role to play, since habits formed in childhood tend to last a lifetime.", vocab: [["part of everyday life", "một phần của cuộc sống hằng ngày"], ["cycle lanes", "làn xe đạp"], ["have a big role to play", "đóng vai trò quan trọng"], ["last a lifetime", "kéo dài suốt đời"]] },
  "Do you think team sports teach more valuable lessons than individual sports?": { a: "I'd say they teach different lessons rather than more valuable ones. Team sports are excellent for learning cooperation and communication, because you have to rely on others and put the team's interests before your own. Individual sports, on the other hand, build self-discipline and responsibility, since there's nobody else to blame if things go wrong. A tennis player, for example, has to solve problems alone during a match. Personally, I think children benefit from trying both, because the ability to work in a team and the ability to motivate yourself are equally important in adult life.", vocab: [["put A before B", "đặt A lên trên B"], ["self-discipline", "tính kỷ luật tự giác"], ["nobody else to blame", "không thể đổ lỗi cho ai khác"]] },

  /* ===== Ẩm thực & Nấu ăn ===== */
  "What is your favourite dish, and how often do you eat it?": { a: "My all-time favourite is bun cha, which is grilled pork served with rice noodles, fresh herbs and a sweet and sour dipping sauce. It's a Hanoi speciality. I probably have it once a week, usually for lunch, since there's a tiny place near my university that does an amazing version. The smell of the grilled pork is irresistible.", vocab: [["all-time favourite", "món yêu thích nhất mọi thời"], ["a speciality", "đặc sản"], ["irresistible", "khó cưỡng lại"]] },
  "Who does most of the cooking in your family?": { a: "It's definitely my mum. She's a fantastic cook, and she takes real pride in preparing proper family meals, even after a long day at work. My dad is in charge of the weekend barbecues, though, and he's quite competitive about it. Whenever I'm home, I try to help out in the kitchen and pick up a few of her recipes.", vocab: [["take pride in", "tự hào về"], ["be in charge of", "phụ trách"], ["pick up", "học lỏm, học được"]] },
  "Do you prefer eating at home or in restaurants?": { a: "On a day-to-day basis, I prefer eating at home. It's healthier and cheaper, and I know exactly what's in my food. But eating out is great for special occasions or catching up with friends, because nobody has to do the washing up. I'd say I eat out about twice a week, mostly at cheap local places.", vocab: [["on a day-to-day basis", "hằng ngày"], ["eat out", "ăn ngoài"], ["do the washing up", "rửa bát"]] },
  "Is there any food you disliked as a child but enjoy now?": { a: "Yes, bitter melon. When I was a kid, my mum used to make a soup with it, and I absolutely hated the bitter taste. I'd try to hide the pieces under my rice. But somewhere along the way, I developed a taste for it, and now I actually find it refreshing, especially in summer. My mum finds that hilarious.", vocab: [["somewhere along the way", "dần dần lúc nào không hay"], ["develop a taste for", "bắt đầu thích (món gì)"], ["refreshing", "thanh mát, sảng khoái"]] },
  "Describe a memorable meal you had with other people.": { a: "The meal I'd like to describe is a family dinner we had at my grandparents' house in the countryside on the first evening of Tet, the Lunar New Year, two years ago.\n\nIt took place in their old house, which has a large wooden table in the main room, right next to the family altar. That year was special because, for the first time in a long while, the whole extended family managed to get together. My uncle's family had flown back from Germany, so there were about twenty of us altogether, including several cousins I hadn't seen since I was a child.\n\nThe food was a traditional Tet feast. There was banh chung, the square sticky rice cake that we'd wrapped together the day before, boiled chicken, spring rolls, pickled onions and a big pot of bamboo shoot soup. My grandmother had been cooking for two days, and she wouldn't let anyone else take charge of the kitchen, although we were allowed to help with the chopping. Everything was delicious, but what I remember most is how lively it was. People were talking over each other, the kids were running around, and my uncle kept telling embarrassing stories about my mum when she was young.\n\nThe reason this meal was so memorable is that it brought everyone together after years apart. My grandfather gave a short speech at the start, and he became quite emotional, saying it was the happiest day he'd had in years. Sadly, he passed away the following spring, so looking back, that dinner was the last time we were all together with him. That's why it means so much to me now, and why I treasure the photos we took that evening.", vocab: [["the extended family", "đại gia đình"], ["a feast", "bữa tiệc thịnh soạn"], ["take charge of", "đảm nhiệm, nắm quyền"], ["treasure", "trân trọng, nâng niu"]] },
  "Why are fewer young people learning to cook at home?": { a: "I think it's mainly about convenience and time. Young people today are often busy with study or work, and food delivery apps make it incredibly easy to order a cheap meal in minutes. In big cities, many also live in small rented rooms without a proper kitchen. Another factor is that parents sometimes prefer their children to focus on studying, so they don't involve them in cooking. Having said that, I've noticed cooking videos are hugely popular on social media, so perhaps the interest is still there. It's just that the practical opportunities are fewer.", vocab: [["food delivery apps", "ứng dụng giao đồ ăn"], ["involve someone in", "cho ai tham gia vào"], ["the interest is still there", "sự quan tâm vẫn còn"]] },
  "How has fast food affected eating habits in your country?": { a: "Quite noticeably, especially in big cities. Twenty years ago, there were very few international fast-food chains in Vietnam, but now you can find fried chicken and burger outlets in almost every shopping centre. Many children see these places as a treat, and some families eat there regularly, which has probably contributed to rising rates of childhood obesity. That said, I don't think fast food has replaced traditional eating habits completely. Vietnamese street food is still cheap and everywhere, and in a sense, dishes like pho or banh mi are our own version of fast food, only usually healthier.", vocab: [["fast-food chains", "chuỗi cửa hàng đồ ăn nhanh"], ["a treat", "món chiêu đãi, phần thưởng"], ["childhood obesity", "béo phì ở trẻ em"]] },
  "Should schools teach children about nutrition and cooking?": { a: "Yes, I strongly believe they should. Cooking is a basic life skill, yet many young people leave home without knowing how to prepare a simple, healthy meal. If children learn about nutrition early, they're more likely to make sensible food choices and less likely to rely on processed food later. Practical classes could also be fun and hands-on, which is a nice change from sitting at a desk. The main challenge, I suppose, is that schools would need kitchens and trained staff, which costs money. But in the long run, it could save a lot on healthcare.", vocab: [["a basic life skill", "kỹ năng sống cơ bản"], ["make sensible food choices", "lựa chọn thực phẩm hợp lý"], ["hands-on", "thực hành"]] },
  "Do you think the food we eat is a key part of our cultural identity?": { a: "Absolutely. Food is one of the most direct ways a culture expresses itself, because it reflects a country's history, geography and values. In Vietnam, for example, the custom of sharing dishes from the middle of the table says a lot about how much we value family and community. Food is also closely connected to festivals, like banh chung at Tet. And when people live abroad, cooking their traditional dishes is often how they stay connected to their roots. Of course, cuisines change over time and borrow from each other, but I think that's part of what keeps culture alive.", vocab: [["cultural identity", "bản sắc văn hóa"], ["say a lot about", "nói lên nhiều điều về"], ["stay connected to their roots", "giữ kết nối với cội nguồn"]] },

  /* ===== Mua sắm ===== */
  "Do you enjoy going shopping?": { a: "It depends on what I'm shopping for. I really enjoy browsing in bookshops or second-hand clothes stores, because you never know what you'll find. But I can't stand shopping for necessities like groceries, especially in crowded supermarkets at the weekend. For things like that, I just want to get in and out as quickly as possible.", vocab: [["browse", "xem hàng, dạo xem"], ["can't stand", "không chịu nổi"], ["get in and out", "vào rồi ra thật nhanh"]] },
  "Do you prefer shopping online or in physical stores?": { a: "For most everyday items, I prefer shopping online because it's so convenient and I can easily compare prices. But for clothes and shoes, I'd rather go to a physical store, since I like to try things on first. I've had a few bad experiences ordering clothes online that looked nothing like the photos, so I've learnt my lesson.", vocab: [["compare prices", "so sánh giá"], ["try something on", "mặc thử"], ["learn my lesson", "rút ra bài học"]] },
  "What was the last thing you bought for yourself?": { a: "The last thing I bought for myself was a pair of running shoes, about two weeks ago. My old ones were completely worn out, and I'd started getting a bit of knee pain. I spent quite a while reading reviews before choosing a pair, and they were a bit pricey, but they're so comfortable that I think it was money well spent.", vocab: [["worn out", "bị mòn, sờn"], ["pricey", "đắt"], ["money well spent", "tiền tiêu đáng giá"]] },
  "Do you usually plan what to buy before you go shopping?": { a: "For food shopping, yes, I always make a list on my phone, otherwise I end up buying lots of snacks I don't need. But when it comes to clothes or things for my room, I'm much less organised. I often go in without a plan and come out with something I hadn't intended to buy, which isn't great for my budget.", vocab: [["make a list", "lập danh sách"], ["when it comes to", "khi nói đến"], ["go in without a plan", "đi (mua sắm) mà không có kế hoạch"]] },
  "Describe something you bought that you were very happy with.": { a: "Something I bought that I've been really happy with is a second-hand bicycle, which I got at the start of my second year at university.\n\nI bought it from a small bike shop tucked away in a side street near Long Bien Bridge in Hanoi. I'd seen it advertised on a local online marketplace, so I went to have a look one Saturday morning. The owner was a friendly older man who'd been repairing bikes for decades, and he let me take it for a test ride around the neighbourhood. It's a classic Japanese city bike, with a basket at the front and a bell, and although it's a bit old, it was in excellent condition.\n\nIt cost me one million eight hundred thousand dong, which is about seventy US dollars. Honestly, that felt like quite a lot at the time, as I was a student living on a tight budget, but a brand-new bike of similar quality would have cost at least three times as much. I managed to negotiate the price down a little, and the owner even threw in a lock and a free tune-up.\n\nThe reason I'm so pleased with it is that it's completely changed how I get around. Before, I relied on ride-hailing apps or took the bus, which was expensive and often slow during rush hour. Now I cycle to class most days, so I save money and get some exercise without even thinking about it. It's also given me a new way to explore the city. At weekends, I sometimes cycle around West Lake or through the Old Quarter early in the morning, and I've discovered so many little cafés and streets I'd never noticed before. For such a small investment, it's made a huge difference to my daily life.", vocab: [["take something for a test ride", "chạy thử (xe)"], ["live on a tight budget", "sống với ngân sách eo hẹp"], ["negotiate the price down", "mặc cả giảm giá"], ["throw in", "tặng kèm"]] },
  "Why do people sometimes buy things they do not really need?": { a: "I think there are both psychological and social reasons. Shopping can give people a quick emotional boost, so some buy things to cheer themselves up after a stressful day, which is often called retail therapy. Clever marketing also plays a huge role, with limited-time offers and flash sales creating a sense of urgency. On top of that, social media puts pressure on people to keep up with trends or show off a certain lifestyle. And with online shopping, buying something takes just a couple of clicks, so there's very little time to stop and think about whether we actually need it.", vocab: [["an emotional boost", "sự phấn chấn tinh thần"], ["retail therapy", "mua sắm để giải tỏa tâm trạng"], ["a sense of urgency", "cảm giác cấp bách"], ["keep up with trends", "theo kịp xu hướng"]] },
  "How does advertising influence what young people choose to buy?": { a: "Quite powerfully, I think, though often in subtle ways. Nowadays, much of the advertising aimed at young people comes through influencers on TikTok or Instagram, so it feels more like a recommendation from a friend than an advert. That makes it more persuasive. Ads also tend to link products with an identity, suggesting that if you wear a certain brand, you'll be cool or successful. My younger sister, for example, once bought an expensive skincare product just because her favourite influencer used it. That said, many young people are becoming more sceptical and check reviews before they buy.", vocab: [["in subtle ways", "một cách tinh vi"], ["link A with B", "gắn A với B"], ["sceptical", "hoài nghi"]] },
  "Will traditional shops disappear because of online shopping?": { a: "I doubt they'll disappear entirely, but many will have to change. Online shopping is clearly winning when it comes to price and convenience, and we've already seen lots of small shops close. However, people still value being able to see, touch and try products before buying, especially clothes and furniture. Shopping is also a social activity for many people. I think successful shops in the future will focus on experience, for example offering cafés, workshops or personalised advice, rather than just selling goods. Small local shops with loyal customers may survive too, because they offer something the internet can't.", vocab: [["disappear entirely", "biến mất hoàn toàn"], ["focus on experience", "tập trung vào trải nghiệm"], ["loyal customers", "khách hàng trung thành"]] },
  "Is a consumer society good or bad for the environment?": { a: "I'd say it's largely harmful, although it's a complicated issue. A society built on constantly buying new things inevitably uses huge amounts of resources and energy, and creates mountains of waste. Fast fashion is a good example, since cheap clothes are often worn only a few times before being thrown away. On the other hand, consumer demand has also driven innovation, such as electric cars and more efficient appliances. So I think the problem isn't consumption itself, but excessive and careless consumption. If people bought fewer, better-quality products and repaired things more, the environmental impact could be much smaller.", vocab: [["mountains of waste", "hàng núi rác thải"], ["fast fashion", "thời trang nhanh"], ["drive innovation", "thúc đẩy đổi mới"], ["environmental impact", "tác động môi trường"]] },

  /* ===== Thời tiết & Các mùa ===== */
  "What is the weather usually like where you live?": { a: "Hanoi has four fairly distinct seasons, which surprises some people. Summers are hot and humid, often over thirty-five degrees, with heavy thunderstorms. Winters, on the other hand, can be surprisingly chilly and damp. The best times of year are definitely spring and autumn, when it's mild and pleasant, and the air is a bit cleaner.", vocab: [["distinct seasons", "các mùa rõ rệt"], ["hot and humid", "nóng ẩm"], ["chilly and damp", "lạnh và ẩm ướt"]] },
  "Which season do you like best, and why?": { a: "I love autumn in Hanoi. The weather is cool and dry, and there's a gentle breeze, which is a welcome relief after the scorching summer. There's also a special atmosphere in the city, with the smell of milk flowers in the evening and street vendors selling green sticky rice. It always puts me in a good mood.", vocab: [["a welcome relief", "sự giải tỏa dễ chịu"], ["scorching", "nóng như thiêu đốt"], ["put me in a good mood", "khiến tôi vui vẻ"]] },
  "Does the weather affect your mood?": { a: "Yes, quite a lot, actually. When it's sunny and mild, I feel energetic and want to be outdoors. But during long stretches of grey, drizzly weather in late winter, I tend to feel a bit sluggish and unmotivated. I've learnt that getting outside for a walk, even on gloomy days, helps lift my spirits.", vocab: [["long stretches of", "những khoảng thời gian dài"], ["sluggish", "uể oải"], ["lift my spirits", "nâng cao tinh thần"]] },
  "Do you check the weather forecast every day?": { a: "Not every day, but most days, especially during the rainy season. I usually glance at the weather app before leaving home, so I know whether to bring a raincoat. It's essential when you ride a motorbike, because getting caught in a sudden downpour is no fun at all. In autumn, when the weather's settled, I check it far less often.", vocab: [["get caught in", "bị mắc kẹt trong"], ["a sudden downpour", "trận mưa rào bất chợt"], ["the weather's settled", "thời tiết ổn định"]] },
  "Describe a time when the weather changed your plans.": { a: "I'd like to talk about a time last September, when a typhoon completely ruined a trip I'd been looking forward to for months.\n\nMy friends and I had planned a long weekend in Quang Binh, in central Vietnam, to go caving in Phong Nha. We'd booked everything weeks in advance: the train tickets, a homestay and a guided tour through one of the caves. Five of us were going, and we'd even made a group chat full of photos and plans. It was meant to be a little celebration after a very stressful exam period.\n\nUnfortunately, about two days before we were due to leave, the forecast showed that a strong typhoon was heading straight for the central coast. At first, we hoped it might change direction, but by the day before our departure, the authorities had issued warnings, and the tour company called to say all cave trips were cancelled for safety reasons. On the day we should have been travelling, it was pouring with rain even in Hanoi, and the news was full of pictures of flooded roads and fallen trees in the central provinces.\n\nAs for how I felt, I was really disappointed at first. We'd put so much effort into planning, and we lost some of the money we'd paid. But after a while, I started to see things differently. When I saw the damage the storm caused, I realised that our cancelled trip was a very small problem compared with what local people were going through. In the end, we made the best of it by having a cosy weekend at a friend's house, cooking and watching films, and a few of us donated to a flood relief fund. We finally made the caving trip the following spring, and the weather was perfect.", vocab: [["look forward to", "mong chờ"], ["be heading straight for", "đang tiến thẳng về phía"], ["pour with rain", "mưa như trút"], ["make the best of it", "tận dụng tốt nhất tình huống"]] },
  "How does the climate of a country influence the lifestyle of its people?": { a: "In many ways, I think. Climate affects everything from what people eat and wear to how they design their homes and spend their free time. In hot countries like Vietnam, for instance, a lot of daily life takes place outdoors, on the pavement or in open-air markets, and people often start work early to avoid the midday heat. In colder countries, social life tends to happen indoors, and homes are built to keep the heat in. Climate can even shape a country's economy, through farming and tourism. Of course, modern technology like air conditioning has reduced this influence to some extent.", vocab: [["take place outdoors", "diễn ra ngoài trời"], ["the midday heat", "cái nóng giữa trưa"], ["shape a country's economy", "định hình nền kinh tế một quốc gia"]] },
  "Do you think weather forecasts are reliable enough to plan around?": { a: "Generally, yes, at least for the next few days. Forecasts have improved enormously thanks to satellites and computer models, so a forecast for tomorrow is usually quite accurate. I'd certainly trust it when deciding whether to take an umbrella. However, the further ahead you look, the less reliable they become, so I wouldn't plan an outdoor wedding based on a forecast two weeks away. Tropical weather can also be unpredictable, with sudden storms appearing out of nowhere. So I'd say forecasts are a useful guide, but it's always wise to have a backup plan.", vocab: [["the further ahead you look", "càng nhìn xa về sau"], ["out of nowhere", "bất ngờ, không báo trước"], ["a backup plan", "kế hoạch dự phòng"]] },
  "Why does extreme weather seem to be happening more often nowadays?": { a: "The main reason, according to most scientists, is climate change. As global temperatures rise, the atmosphere holds more moisture and energy, which can lead to more intense storms, heavier rainfall and longer heatwaves. In Vietnam, for example, typhoons and floods in the central region seem to be getting more severe. That said, part of the reason it seems more frequent may be that we hear about it more. Thanks to the internet and social media, we now see dramatic images of floods or wildfires from all over the world almost instantly, which makes these events feel more common.", vocab: [["global temperatures rise", "nhiệt độ toàn cầu tăng"], ["heatwaves", "các đợt nắng nóng"], ["more severe", "nghiêm trọng hơn"]] },
  "How should cities prepare for heatwaves or floods in the future?": { a: "I think cities need both short-term and long-term measures. For heatwaves, planting more trees and creating green spaces can significantly cool down urban areas, and buildings could be designed with better insulation and shading. Cities should also set up cooling centres for vulnerable people, like the elderly. As for floods, improving drainage systems is essential, since many streets in Hanoi flood after just one heavy downpour. Some cities, such as Rotterdam, have even built parks that double as water reservoirs. Above all, I believe planners have to stop building on natural floodplains, otherwise the problem will only get worse.", vocab: [["drainage systems", "hệ thống thoát nước"], ["vulnerable people", "những người dễ bị tổn thương"], ["double as", "kiêm luôn chức năng"], ["floodplains", "vùng bãi bồi, vùng ngập lũ"]] },

  /* ===== Giao thông & Đi lại ===== */
  "How do you usually get to work or school?": { a: "These days, I usually cycle to university, which takes about twenty minutes. It's cheaper than taking a motorbike taxi, and it's a nice way to wake myself up in the morning. When it's pouring with rain, though, I give in and book a ride through an app, because arriving soaking wet isn't ideal.", vocab: [["wake myself up", "làm mình tỉnh táo"], ["give in", "chịu thua, nhượng bộ"], ["soaking wet", "ướt sũng"]] },
  "How long does your daily journey take?": { a: "Normally, it takes me around twenty minutes each way, which isn't bad at all for Hanoi. However, if I leave even ten minutes late, I get caught in rush-hour traffic, and the journey can easily double. So I've got into the habit of setting off early, and I often use the spare time to grab breakfast near campus.", vocab: [["each way", "mỗi chiều"], ["get into the habit of", "hình thành thói quen"], ["set off", "khởi hành"]] },
  "Do you ever use public transport?": { a: "Yes, occasionally. I take the bus when I need to go somewhere far across the city, and recently I've started using the new metro line, which is clean, air-conditioned and surprisingly punctual. To be honest, I'd use public transport more often if the network covered more areas, because at the moment many routes are quite limited.", vocab: [["air-conditioned", "có điều hòa"], ["punctual", "đúng giờ"], ["cover more areas", "phủ rộng hơn"]] },
  "Would you like to learn to drive, or do you drive already?": { a: "I can ride a motorbike, of course, like almost everyone in Vietnam, but I can't drive a car yet. I'm planning to take driving lessons after I graduate, because it would be really handy for family trips. That said, I'm a bit nervous about driving in Hanoi, since the traffic can be pretty chaotic.", vocab: [["take driving lessons", "học lái xe"], ["handy", "tiện lợi"], ["chaotic", "hỗn loạn"]] },
  "Describe a journey you made that took longer than expected.": { a: "I'd like to talk about a bus journey I made from Hanoi to Sa Pa, in the mountains of northern Vietnam, about two years ago. It was supposed to take around six hours, but in the end it took almost eleven.\n\nI was travelling with my cousin to visit a friend of ours who was teaching English in a village near Sa Pa. We'd booked seats on an overnight sleeper bus, which was meant to leave Hanoi at ten in the evening and arrive early the next morning, giving us a whole day to explore.\n\nThe first part of the journey went smoothly. We left on time, the roads were quiet, and I actually managed to get some sleep. But at around three in the morning, I woke up and realised the bus had stopped. It turned out that heavy rain had caused a landslide on the mountain road ahead, and rocks and mud were blocking both lanes. There was a long line of buses and lorries stuck behind us, and nobody knew how long it would take to clear.\n\nAs for how I dealt with the situation, to begin with, I felt quite anxious, especially because the phone signal was very weak and I couldn't contact our friend. But my cousin was very calm, and we decided there was no point in worrying about something we couldn't control. Once it got light, we got off the bus, chatted with other passengers and shared our snacks with a family from Lao Cai. One of the drivers even made instant noodles for everyone on a little gas stove. Eventually, after about five hours, a bulldozer cleared the road and we continued. Looking back, it was frustrating, but it taught me to be more patient and flexible when travelling.", vocab: [["go smoothly", "diễn ra suôn sẻ"], ["a landslide", "vụ sạt lở đất"], ["there's no point in", "chẳng ích gì khi"], ["patient and flexible", "kiên nhẫn và linh hoạt"]] },
  "What are the main causes of traffic congestion in big cities?": { a: "I'd say the root cause is that the number of vehicles has grown much faster than the road network. In Hanoi, for instance, there are millions of motorbikes and an increasing number of private cars, yet many roads are still narrow. Poor urban planning makes it worse, because offices, schools and shopping centres are concentrated in the same districts, so everyone travels in the same direction at the same time. Public transport has also been underdeveloped until recently. And finally, things like illegal parking and road works can easily turn a busy street into a complete standstill.", vocab: [["the root cause", "nguyên nhân gốc rễ"], ["urban planning", "quy hoạch đô thị"], ["underdeveloped", "kém phát triển"], ["a complete standstill", "tình trạng đứng im hoàn toàn"]] },
  "Should private cars be banned from city centres?": { a: "I'm in favour of restricting them, but a complete ban might be too extreme in some cities. Car-free centres, like those in parts of Oslo or Madrid, have shown that they can reduce pollution, make streets safer and give space back to pedestrians and cafés. However, for a ban to work, there has to be a reliable public transport alternative, otherwise people simply can't get where they need to go. You also have to think about elderly or disabled people and delivery vehicles. So I'd suggest a gradual approach, starting with pedestrian zones at weekends and expanding them over time.", vocab: [["too extreme", "quá cực đoan"], ["give space back to pedestrians", "trả lại không gian cho người đi bộ"], ["a gradual approach", "cách tiếp cận từng bước"]] },
  "How might self-driving vehicles change the way we travel?": { a: "Potentially, they could make a huge difference. If they become reliable, they might dramatically reduce accidents, since most crashes are caused by human error such as speeding or tiredness. They could also give independence to people who can't drive, like the elderly. Commuting might change too, because people could work or rest during the journey. However, I'm a little sceptical about how soon this will happen, especially in countries with unpredictable traffic like Vietnam, where motorbikes weave in all directions. There are also serious questions about safety, jobs for drivers and who's responsible when something goes wrong.", vocab: [["human error", "lỗi do con người"], ["give independence to", "mang lại sự tự chủ cho"], ["be sceptical about", "hoài nghi về"]] },
  "Why do some people still prefer driving even when public transport is available?": { a: "I think the main reasons are convenience and control. With a car or motorbike, you can leave whenever you like and go directly from door to door, without waiting or changing lines. This matters a lot for people with children or heavy shopping. Comfort is another factor, especially in hot weather, when standing on a crowded bus isn't very appealing. For some people, owning a car is also a status symbol. That said, I think attitudes are slowly changing, and if public transport becomes faster and more comfortable, more people will probably make the switch.", vocab: [["from door to door", "từ cửa đến cửa"], ["a status symbol", "biểu tượng địa vị"], ["make the switch", "chuyển đổi"]] },

  /* ===== Bạn bè & Các mối quan hệ ===== */
  "Do you have a large or a small group of friends?": { a: "I'd say I have quite a small group of close friends, about four or five people I've known since high school. I get along with lots of people at university, but those old friends are the ones I really open up to. I prefer it that way, because I'd rather have a few deep friendships than many casual ones.", vocab: [["get along with", "hòa hợp với"], ["open up to", "cởi mở, tâm sự với"], ["casual friendships", "những mối quan hệ bạn bè xã giao"]] },
  "How do you usually keep in touch with your friends?": { a: "Mostly through messaging apps. My close friends and I have a group chat that's active pretty much all day, where we share funny videos and news. Since we live in different cities now, we also have video calls every few weeks. Nothing beats meeting in person, though, so we always try to catch up when we're all home for Tet.", vocab: [["a group chat", "nhóm trò chuyện"], ["nothing beats", "không gì sánh bằng"], ["catch up", "gặp gỡ hàn huyên"]] },
  "What do you and your friends like doing together?": { a: "We're big foodies, so most of our meetups revolve around eating. We love hunting down new street food stalls or trying a trendy café. Apart from that, we sometimes go to karaoke, which always ends with everyone singing terribly and laughing a lot. Occasionally, we organise short weekend trips out of the city as well.", vocab: [["a foodie", "người sành ăn"], ["hunt down", "lùng tìm"], ["a trendy café", "quán cà phê thời thượng"]] },
  "Is it easy for you to make new friends?": { a: "Not particularly, to be honest. I'm a bit shy at first, so I find it hard to start conversations with strangers. But once I get to know someone, I'm quite chatty and loyal. I've found that joining clubs helps a lot, because when you share a common interest, the conversation flows much more naturally.", vocab: [["get to know someone", "làm quen, hiểu ai đó"], ["a common interest", "sở thích chung"], ["the conversation flows", "cuộc trò chuyện trôi chảy"]] },
  "Describe a friend who has had a positive influence on you.": { a: "The friend I'd like to talk about is Minh, who has been one of my closest friends for about six years now.\n\nWe met in the first week of high school, when we were assigned to sit next to each other in maths class. To be honest, my first impression wasn't great. He seemed very serious and hardly said a word. But a few weeks later, we were put in the same group for a science project, and I discovered he had a brilliant sense of humour and was incredibly kind. We hit it off straight away after that, and we've been inseparable ever since. Even though we now go to different universities, we still talk almost every day.\n\nWhen we're together, we usually do quite simple things. We go for long walks around the lake, play badminton or sit in a café discussing everything from books to our plans for the future. In the summer, we often go on short trips together, and last year we volunteered at a summer camp for children in a rural area.\n\nThe main way he has influenced me is by showing me the value of perseverance. Minh comes from a fairly poor family, and he's had to work part-time since he was sixteen while still getting excellent grades. Yet he never complains or makes excuses. Whenever I feel like giving up on something, I think about how hard he works, and it gives me the push I need. He's also taught me to be more generous with my time. It was actually his idea to volunteer at that summer camp, and it turned out to be one of the most rewarding things I've ever done. I genuinely think I'm a better person because of him.", vocab: [["hit it off", "hợp nhau ngay"], ["be inseparable", "không thể tách rời"], ["perseverance", "sự kiên trì"], ["give someone the push they need", "tiếp thêm động lực cần thiết cho ai"]] },
  "Are online friendships as meaningful as face-to-face ones?": { a: "I think they can be, but they're usually different. Online friendships can be very meaningful, especially for people who feel isolated or who share niche interests that nobody around them understands. Some people even find it easier to open up online. However, I'd argue that face-to-face friendships tend to be deeper in many ways, because physical presence matters. When you're going through a hard time, a friend who can sit with you or bring you food offers a kind of support that messages can't fully replace. Ideally, many online friendships eventually move offline anyway.", vocab: [["feel isolated", "cảm thấy cô lập"], ["niche interests", "sở thích riêng biệt, ít phổ biến"], ["go through a hard time", "trải qua giai đoạn khó khăn"]] },
  "Why do some friendships last a lifetime while others fade?": { a: "I think it largely comes down to effort and shared values. Friendships that last usually involve people who make time for each other, even when life gets busy, and who are willing to forgive mistakes. Having similar values also helps, because interests can change, but core beliefs tend to stay the same. On the other hand, many friendships are based on circumstances, like being in the same class or office. When those circumstances change, for example when someone moves away or starts a family, the friendship can naturally drift apart. And honestly, I don't think that's always a bad thing.", vocab: [["shared values", "giá trị chung"], ["core beliefs", "niềm tin cốt lõi"], ["drift apart", "dần xa cách"]] },
  "Is it more important to have many friends or a few close ones?": { a: "Personally, I believe a few close friends are far more important. Close friends are the people who really know you, who you can rely on in a crisis and who will tell you honestly when you're making a mistake. Research also suggests that the quality of our relationships matters more for wellbeing than the quantity. That said, having a wider social circle has its advantages too. It can open up job opportunities and expose you to different perspectives. So I'd say the ideal is a small inner circle of close friends, supported by a broader network of acquaintances.", vocab: [["rely on someone in a crisis", "dựa vào ai lúc khó khăn"], ["a wider social circle", "vòng quan hệ rộng hơn"], ["an inner circle", "nhóm thân cận"], ["acquaintances", "người quen"]] },
  "How do relationships between neighbours today compare with those in the past?": { a: "In my country, I think they've become noticeably more distant, particularly in cities. When my parents were young, neighbours were almost like extended family. They'd share food, look after each other's children and help out with weddings or funerals. Nowadays, many people live in apartment blocks and work long hours, so they hardly know who lives next door. That said, the picture isn't entirely negative. In smaller towns and traditional alleys, strong neighbourly bonds still exist, and during the pandemic, many neighbours supported each other through online community groups, which was quite heart-warming to see.", vocab: [["noticeably more distant", "xa cách rõ rệt hơn"], ["look after", "trông nom, chăm sóc"], ["neighbourly bonds", "tình làng nghĩa xóm"], ["heart-warming", "ấm lòng"]] },

  /* ===== Nghệ thuật & Bảo tàng ===== */
  "Do you like looking at paintings or other works of art?": { a: "Yes, I do, although I'm definitely not an expert. I especially enjoy landscape paintings and photography, because they can transport you to another place. I don't always understand modern abstract art, to be honest, but I still find it interesting to stand in front of a piece and think about what the artist was trying to say.", vocab: [["works of art", "tác phẩm nghệ thuật"], ["transport you to another place", "đưa bạn đến một nơi khác"], ["abstract art", "nghệ thuật trừu tượng"]] },
  "When was the last time you visited a museum or gallery?": { a: "The last time was about two months ago, when I went to the Vietnam Women's Museum in Hanoi with a friend. I'd been meaning to go for ages. It was really well organised, with lots of personal stories and photos, and I came away with a much deeper appreciation of the role women have played in our history.", vocab: [["have been meaning to", "định làm từ lâu"], ["come away with", "ra về với (cảm nhận gì)"], ["a deeper appreciation of", "sự trân trọng sâu sắc hơn đối với"]] },
  "Is there any art on the walls of your home?": { a: "Not much, actually, since I'm renting and I don't want to damage the walls. But I do have a couple of framed prints above my desk, including a watercolour of Hoi An that I bought from a street artist there. It reminds me of a great trip, and it brightens up the room. My parents' house has much more art.", vocab: [["framed prints", "tranh in đóng khung"], ["a watercolour", "tranh màu nước"], ["brighten up", "làm sáng sủa, tươi tắn hơn"]] },
  "Did you enjoy art lessons at school?": { a: "Yes, I did, mostly because they were a break from the more academic subjects. I wasn't particularly gifted, but I enjoyed experimenting with colours and materials. Unfortunately, art was seen as a minor subject at my school, so we only had one lesson a week, and it was often replaced by extra maths before exams.", vocab: [["a break from", "khoảng nghỉ khỏi"], ["gifted", "có năng khiếu"], ["a minor subject", "môn phụ"]] },
  "Describe a museum, gallery or exhibition you have visited.": { a: "I'd like to talk about the Vietnam Museum of Ethnology in Hanoi, which I visited for the first time about a year ago.\n\nIt's located in Cau Giay district, a little way from the city centre, so it's not somewhere tourists usually stumble upon by accident. I went there one Saturday afternoon with two classmates, partly because we were doing a group presentation about cultural diversity and needed some material, and partly because I'd heard great things about it from friends.\n\nThe museum is divided into two main areas. Indoors, there are galleries introducing the fifty-four ethnic groups in Vietnam, with displays of traditional clothing, musical instruments, tools and jewellery, as well as short videos showing ceremonies and daily life. Outdoors, there's a large garden where full-size traditional houses have been rebuilt, including a tall communal house from the Central Highlands and a stilt house from the north-west. You can actually walk inside them, which makes it feel much more real than just looking at objects behind glass.\n\nWhat I found most interesting was a small section on the tomb houses of the Gia Rai people, decorated with wooden statues. I'd never known about this tradition before, and reading about how the community celebrates the end of mourning, rather than just grieving, completely changed my understanding of how different cultures view death. I also really appreciated how the museum includes the voices of the communities themselves, through interviews and personal objects, so it doesn't feel like outsiders describing them. I left with a real sense of how culturally rich my own country is, and I realised how little I'd known about the minority groups who are part of it. I've since recommended it to everyone I know.", vocab: [["stumble upon", "tình cờ bắt gặp"], ["cultural diversity", "sự đa dạng văn hóa"], ["a stilt house", "nhà sàn"], ["culturally rich", "giàu bản sắc văn hóa"]] },
  "Should museums be free for everyone to visit?": { a: "I think there's a strong case for making them free, or at least very cheap. Museums preserve a nation's history and culture, so everyone should have the opportunity to learn from them, regardless of income. In the UK, for example, many national museums are free, and they attract huge numbers of visitors, including families and students who might not otherwise go. However, museums are expensive to run, and if they don't charge entry fees, the money has to come from taxes or donations. A sensible compromise might be free entry for locals and students, while foreign tourists pay a fee.", vocab: [["there's a strong case for", "có lý do xác đáng để"], ["regardless of income", "bất kể thu nhập"], ["charge entry fees", "thu phí vào cửa"], ["a sensible compromise", "một giải pháp dung hòa hợp lý"]] },
  "Why do governments spend public money on the arts?": { a: "I think governments see the arts as a public good, similar to parks or libraries. Theatres, museums and festivals help preserve a nation's cultural heritage and give people a shared sense of identity. There are economic reasons too, because a vibrant arts scene attracts tourists and creates jobs. Without public funding, many traditional art forms, like water puppetry in Vietnam, might simply die out, since they can't survive on ticket sales alone. Of course, some people argue that money should go to healthcare or education first. But I'd say a healthy society needs culture as well as hospitals.", vocab: [["a public good", "hàng hóa công, lợi ích chung"], ["cultural heritage", "di sản văn hóa"], ["a vibrant arts scene", "đời sống nghệ thuật sôi động"], ["die out", "mai một, biến mất"]] },
  "How has technology changed the way people experience art?": { a: "Hugely. Probably the biggest change is accessibility. Many major museums now offer virtual tours and high-resolution images online, so someone in a small Vietnamese town can explore the Louvre from their phone. Technology has also created new forms of art, like digital installations where visitors walk through projected images, which are extremely popular with young people. On the other hand, I sometimes feel technology can distract from art. In galleries, lots of visitors seem more interested in taking selfies than actually looking at the paintings. So it's made art more accessible, but perhaps less contemplative.", vocab: [["accessibility", "khả năng tiếp cận"], ["virtual tours", "tham quan ảo"], ["distract from", "làm xao nhãng khỏi"], ["contemplative", "trầm tư, chiêm nghiệm"]] },
  "Is it important for children to learn about the history of their own country through museums?": { a: "Yes, I think it's very valuable. Textbooks can make history feel dry and distant, whereas museums let children see real objects, like weapons, letters or clothes, which makes the past much more tangible. I still remember visiting a war museum on a school trip and being deeply moved by the personal stories. That kind of experience helps children understand where they come from and why certain events still matter. That said, museums work best as a complement to classroom learning, not a replacement. Teachers need to prepare students beforehand and discuss what they saw afterwards.", vocab: [["dry and distant", "khô khan và xa vời"], ["tangible", "hữu hình, cụ thể"], ["be deeply moved", "vô cùng xúc động"], ["a complement to", "sự bổ trợ cho"]] },

  /* ===== Đọc sách ===== */
  "Do you enjoy reading in your free time?": { a: "Yes, I do, although I don't read as much as I'd like to. I usually read for about half an hour before bed, because it helps me switch off from my phone and fall asleep more easily. I mostly read novels and the occasional self-help book. When I'm on holiday, I can easily get through two or three books.", vocab: [["switch off from", "tách khỏi, ngừng dùng"], ["the occasional", "thỉnh thoảng một"], ["get through", "đọc hết, hoàn thành"]] },
  "Do you prefer paper books or e-books?": { a: "I definitely prefer paper books. There's something special about the feel of the pages, and I find it easier to concentrate without notifications popping up. I also like seeing my books lined up on a shelf. That said, e-books are really handy when I'm travelling, because I can carry dozens of them without any extra weight.", vocab: [["the feel of the pages", "cảm giác cầm trang sách"], ["pop up", "hiện lên"], ["lined up on a shelf", "xếp hàng trên kệ"]] },
  "What kind of books did you read as a child?": { a: "As a child, I was a huge fan of comics, especially Doraemon and Detective Conan, which I used to borrow from a little rental shop near my house. I also loved Vietnamese folk tales, which my grandmother used to read to me at bedtime. Those stories taught me a lot about right and wrong without feeling preachy.", vocab: [["a huge fan of", "fan cuồng của"], ["folk tales", "truyện cổ tích dân gian"], ["preachy", "lên lớp, dạy đời"]] },
  "Do you ever read books in English?": { a: "Yes, I've been trying to read more in English to improve my vocabulary. I started with graded readers, which are simplified versions of famous novels, and now I can manage some easier original books. I'm currently reading a mystery novel. It's slow going, and I still look up quite a few words, but it's very rewarding.", vocab: [["graded readers", "sách đọc phân cấp độ"], ["slow going", "tiến triển chậm"], ["look up words", "tra từ"]] },
  "Describe a book that you would recommend to others.": { a: "The book I'd like to recommend is 'Sapiens: A Brief History of Humankind' by Yuval Noah Harari. I actually read the Vietnamese translation first and then the English version later.\n\nIt's a non-fiction book that tells the story of the human species, from our early ancestors in Africa to the present day. What makes it different from a typical history book is that it focuses on big ideas rather than dates and battles. For example, Harari argues that what made humans so successful was our ability to believe in shared stories, such as religions, nations and even money, which allowed huge numbers of strangers to cooperate. He also discusses how farming, empires and science have shaped our lives, and raises questions about where we might be heading in the future.\n\nI read it about three years ago, during the summer before I started university. A friend lent me her copy, and I remember being hooked from the very first chapter. I'd read a few pages each night, but I often ended up staying awake much longer than I'd planned because I wanted to find out what came next.\n\nThe main reason I'd recommend it is that it completely changes the way you look at the world. It made me question things I'd always taken for granted, like why we value money or why we organise societies the way we do. It's also written in a very accessible and entertaining style, with lots of humour and surprising examples, so you don't need any background in history to enjoy it. Of course, some experts disagree with certain arguments in the book, but I think that's part of its value, because it encourages readers to think critically and form their own opinions.", vocab: [["be hooked", "bị cuốn hút"], ["take something for granted", "coi điều gì là hiển nhiên"], ["accessible", "dễ tiếp cận, dễ hiểu"], ["think critically", "tư duy phản biện"]] },
  "Why do you think fewer people read books for pleasure nowadays?": { a: "I think the main reason is the competition for our attention. Streaming services, video games and social media offer instant entertainment, which requires much less effort than reading a novel. Many of us have also got used to consuming information in short bursts, so sitting down with a long book can feel quite demanding. Busy lifestyles play a role as well, since people often feel too tired to read after work. That said, I'm not sure reading is dying out entirely. Audiobooks are increasingly popular, and online book communities have actually encouraged many young people to start reading again.", vocab: [["the competition for our attention", "sự tranh giành sự chú ý của chúng ta"], ["in short bursts", "theo từng đợt ngắn"], ["die out", "biến mất dần"]] },
  "Will printed books eventually be replaced by digital formats?": { a: "I don't think they'll be completely replaced, at least not in the foreseeable future. Digital formats are clearly growing, because e-books are cheaper, portable and instantly available. Textbooks and reference books in particular may well become mostly digital. However, many readers, including young people, still prefer the physical experience of a printed book. In fact, sales of paper books have remained surprisingly stable in many countries. Printed books also make great gifts and collectibles in a way e-books never will. So my prediction is that the two formats will coexist, with people choosing whichever suits the situation.", vocab: [["in the foreseeable future", "trong tương lai gần"], ["remain stable", "giữ ổn định"], ["coexist", "cùng tồn tại"]] },
  "How can parents encourage children to develop a reading habit?": { a: "I think the most powerful thing parents can do is to lead by example. If children see their parents reading regularly, they're much more likely to see it as a normal and enjoyable activity. Reading aloud together at bedtime is also great, because it builds a positive association between books and family time. Another important point is to let children choose what they read, whether it's comics or adventure stories, rather than forcing 'serious' books on them. Finally, parents could limit screen time a little and take children to libraries or bookshops, so books become part of everyday life.", vocab: [["lead by example", "làm gương"], ["read aloud", "đọc to"], ["a positive association", "sự liên tưởng tích cực"], ["force something on someone", "ép ai phải nhận điều gì"]] },
  "Is reading fiction as valuable as reading factual books?": { a: "I'd argue that it's just as valuable, but in a different way. Factual books are obviously great for gaining knowledge and learning practical skills. Fiction, on the other hand, helps develop empathy, because it lets you see the world through someone else's eyes and understand their emotions. Several studies have suggested that people who read novels are better at understanding others. Fiction can also improve creativity and language skills. Personally, I think a healthy reading diet includes both, much like a balanced meal. Fiction feeds your imagination, while non-fiction feeds your understanding of the real world.", vocab: [["develop empathy", "phát triển sự đồng cảm"], ["see the world through someone else's eyes", "nhìn thế giới qua đôi mắt người khác"], ["a healthy reading diet", "thói quen đọc cân bằng"]] },

  /* ===== Thói quen sống xanh ===== */
  "Do you try to recycle at home?": { a: "Yes, I do my best. My flatmate and I keep separate bags for plastic bottles, cans and paper, and we sell them to a local scrap collector who comes by every week. It isn't a perfect system, because Hanoi doesn't have proper recycling bins everywhere, but at least it keeps some waste out of landfill.", vocab: [["do my best", "cố gắng hết sức"], ["a scrap collector", "người thu mua phế liệu"], ["keep something out of landfill", "giữ cho thứ gì không bị chôn lấp ở bãi rác"]] },
  "Do you usually carry a reusable bag or water bottle?": { a: "Yes, I always carry a stainless steel water bottle in my backpack. Most cafés near my university will even fill it up for free. I'm less consistent with reusable bags, to be honest. I have a few folded up at home, but I often forget them, so I've started keeping one in my motorbike seat.", vocab: [["stainless steel", "thép không gỉ"], ["fill it up", "đổ đầy"], ["be consistent with", "duy trì đều đặn"]] },
  "How much plastic do you think you use every day?": { a: "Probably more than I'd like to admit. Even though I avoid plastic bottles and bags, a lot of food in Vietnam comes wrapped in plastic, especially takeaway meals and street food. If I order a delivery, it often arrives with plastic boxes, cutlery and sauce containers. I'm trying to cut down by eating in more often.", vocab: [["more than I'd like to admit", "nhiều hơn mức tôi muốn thừa nhận"], ["takeaway meals", "đồ ăn mang đi"], ["eat in", "ăn tại chỗ, ăn ở nhà"]] },
  "Have you changed any habits to help the environment?": { a: "Yes, a few. The biggest change is that I switched from riding a motorbike to cycling for short journeys, which cuts down on emissions and saves money. I've also become more careful about electricity, like turning off the air conditioner when I leave the room. They're small changes, but they've become second nature now.", vocab: [["cut down on emissions", "giảm khí thải"], ["become second nature", "trở thành thói quen tự nhiên"], ["turn off", "tắt"]] },
  "Describe something you do to reduce waste in your daily life.": { a: "Something I do to reduce waste is bringing my own lunch box and cutlery whenever I buy takeaway food, instead of accepting disposable plastic or polystyrene containers.\n\nI started doing this about a year and a half ago, after watching a documentary about plastic pollution in the ocean. There was one scene showing a beach in Vietnam covered in food containers and plastic bags, and it really shocked me, because I recognised exactly the kind of packaging I used every single day. That same week, I worked out that I was probably throwing away around ten plastic boxes a week just from lunches, so I decided to do something about it.\n\nNow I keep a stainless steel lunch box, a pair of chopsticks and a spoon in my backpack. When I buy rice or noodles from street vendors near my university, I simply hand them my box. At first, I felt a bit awkward, and a few sellers looked at me strangely. But most of them were actually very supportive, and one lady even gives me a little extra food because, as she puts it, I'm saving her money on packaging.\n\nI'd say it's mostly easy now, but there are still challenges. Sometimes I forget my box, or I'm in a hurry and it's simply more convenient to accept the plastic. Washing the box in the evening can also be a bit of a chore when I'm tired.\n\nI believe it makes a difference for a couple of reasons. On a personal level, I've avoided hundreds of plastic containers that would otherwise have ended up in landfill. More importantly, though, it's had a ripple effect. Several of my classmates noticed what I was doing and started bringing their own boxes too, so the impact is gradually spreading.", vocab: [["disposable plastic", "nhựa dùng một lần"], ["work out", "tính ra"], ["a bit of a chore", "hơi phiền, một việc vặt"], ["a ripple effect", "hiệu ứng lan tỏa"]] },
  "Is protecting the environment mainly the responsibility of individuals or governments?": { a: "I'd say governments bear the main responsibility, although individuals still have an important role. Governments have the power to make changes on a scale that individuals simply can't, for example by investing in renewable energy, setting emission standards for factories or improving public transport. Without those systems in place, it's very hard for ordinary people to live sustainably, however much they want to. That said, governments in democratic countries often respond to public pressure, and consumer choices can push companies to change. So individuals matter, not just through their own habits, but through what they demand from leaders.", vocab: [["bear the main responsibility", "chịu trách nhiệm chính"], ["on a scale", "ở quy mô"], ["public pressure", "áp lực dư luận"], ["live sustainably", "sống bền vững"]] },
  "Why do some people find it hard to change their habits even when they know they should?": { a: "I think it's mainly because habits are automatic, so changing them requires constant effort and attention, at least at first. Convenience is another big obstacle. Using a plastic bag or driving a short distance is simply easier than the alternative, especially when people are busy or tired. There's also the feeling that one person's actions won't make any real difference, which can be quite demotivating. And sometimes the benefits of changing are long-term and invisible, while the costs are immediate. That's why I think making green choices cheaper and easier is more effective than simply telling people what they should do.", vocab: [["a big obstacle", "trở ngại lớn"], ["demotivating", "làm nản lòng"], ["green choices", "lựa chọn thân thiện môi trường"]] },
  "Should companies be forced to reduce the packaging on their products?": { a: "Yes, I think regulation is necessary, because voluntary efforts haven't gone far enough. A lot of packaging is completely unnecessary, like fruit wrapped in plastic or small items in huge boxes, and it creates enormous amounts of waste. If governments set clear limits, or made companies pay for recycling the packaging they produce, businesses would quickly find more efficient solutions. Some European countries already do this. Of course, packaging does serve a purpose, such as keeping food safe and reducing spoilage, so the rules need to be sensible. But overall, I believe the responsibility should lie with producers rather than consumers.", vocab: [["go far enough", "đủ mạnh, đủ triệt để"], ["serve a purpose", "có công dụng"], ["reduce spoilage", "giảm hư hỏng (thực phẩm)"], ["lie with", "thuộc về"]] },
  "How effective are small personal actions compared with large-scale policies?": { a: "In terms of direct impact, large-scale policies are undoubtedly far more effective. A single law banning single-use plastics, for example, can prevent billions of items from being produced, whereas one person refusing plastic bags makes only a tiny difference. However, I don't think personal actions are pointless. When enough people change their behaviour, it creates social norms and shows politicians that there's public support for stronger policies. In that sense, individual actions can pave the way for bigger changes. So I'd see them as complementary rather than competing approaches.", vocab: [["in terms of direct impact", "xét về tác động trực tiếp"], ["social norms", "chuẩn mực xã hội"], ["pave the way for", "mở đường cho"], ["complementary", "bổ sung cho nhau"]] },

  /* ===== Lễ hội & Dịp kỷ niệm ===== */
  "What is the most important festival in your country?": { a: "It's undoubtedly Tet, the Lunar New Year. It usually falls in late January or February, and it's the time when everyone returns to their hometown to be with family. People clean and decorate their houses, visit relatives and give lucky money to children. For most Vietnamese people, it's the highlight of the whole year.", vocab: [["fall in", "rơi vào (thời điểm)"], ["lucky money", "tiền lì xì"], ["the highlight of the year", "dịp nổi bật nhất trong năm"]] },
  "How do you usually celebrate your birthday?": { a: "I usually keep it quite low-key. My family normally has a special dinner at home, and my mum makes my favourite dishes. Then, at the weekend, I go out for dinner or karaoke with a few close friends. I'm not a big fan of huge parties, so a relaxed evening with people I care about suits me perfectly.", vocab: [["keep it low-key", "tổ chức đơn giản, kín đáo"], ["not a big fan of", "không thích lắm"], ["suit me perfectly", "rất hợp với tôi"]] },
  "Do you enjoy going to big celebrations or parties?": { a: "To be honest, only in small doses. I enjoy the atmosphere of a big celebration for an hour or two, especially weddings, where there's lots of food and music. But after a while, I find the noise and crowds quite draining, and I start looking forward to going home. I'm much more comfortable at small gatherings.", vocab: [["in small doses", "với liều lượng nhỏ, một chút thôi"], ["draining", "gây mệt mỏi"], ["small gatherings", "buổi tụ tập nhỏ"]] },
  "Is there a festival you looked forward to as a child?": { a: "Definitely the Mid-Autumn Festival. As a child, I couldn't wait for it every year. My parents would buy me a star-shaped lantern, and I'd parade around the neighbourhood with other kids in the evening. We'd also eat mooncakes and watch lion dances. It's still a festival that fills me with nostalgia.", vocab: [["couldn't wait for", "háo hức mong chờ"], ["parade around", "rước đi quanh"], ["fill me with nostalgia", "khiến tôi đầy hoài niệm"]] },
  "Describe a special celebration you attended.": { a: "The celebration I'd like to describe is my grandparents' fiftieth wedding anniversary, which we celebrated about a year ago.\n\nIt took place at my grandparents' house in a village in Thai Binh province, where they've lived their whole married life. Since the house isn't very big, my uncles put up a large tent in the front yard and hired tables and chairs, so it ended up feeling like a cross between a family reunion and a village festival. Around eighty people came, including relatives from all over the country and lots of neighbours.\n\nThe preparations started days before. On the day itself, the women in the family spent the whole morning cooking a huge feast, while the younger ones like me decorated the yard with flowers and lanterns. In the afternoon, there was a short ceremony where my grandparents lit incense at the family altar to honour their ancestors. Then everyone sat down to eat, and there were speeches from my eldest uncle and from one of my grandfather's oldest friends. Later, the grandchildren performed a few songs, and my cousins and I put together a slideshow of old photos, which made a lot of people laugh and a few people cry.\n\nThe reason it was so special for me is that it made me realise what a remarkable achievement fifty years together is. During the slideshow, my grandmother told the story of how they met during very difficult times after the war, when they had almost nothing. Hearing that, I felt incredibly proud and grateful, because everything our family has today comes from their hard work. It was also lovely to see four generations in one place, which rarely happens nowadays. It's a day I'll remember for the rest of my life.", vocab: [["a cross between", "sự pha trộn giữa"], ["honour their ancestors", "tưởng nhớ tổ tiên"], ["put together", "chuẩn bị, tạo ra"], ["a remarkable achievement", "thành tựu đáng nể"]] },
  "Have traditional festivals lost their original meaning in modern times?": { a: "To some extent, yes. Many festivals have become increasingly commercialised, so the focus has shifted towards shopping, gifts and decorations rather than their spiritual or family meaning. During Tet, for example, some young people now see it mainly as a long holiday and travel abroad instead of visiting relatives. However, I don't think the original meaning has disappeared. Most families still gather together, honour their ancestors and follow traditional customs. In a way, festivals have always evolved over time, so perhaps it's natural that they adapt to modern lifestyles while keeping their core values.", vocab: [["become commercialised", "bị thương mại hóa"], ["shift towards", "chuyển sang"], ["core values", "giá trị cốt lõi"]] },
  "Why do people spend so much money on celebrations such as weddings?": { a: "In my country, I think social pressure is a major factor. A wedding is seen as a reflection of the family's status, so many parents feel they have to invite hundreds of guests and hire an expensive venue to avoid losing face. There's also a sense that it's a once-in-a-lifetime event, so couples want everything to be perfect. Social media has made this even stronger, because people compare their weddings with the glamorous ones they see online. Personally, I think it's a shame when couples start married life in debt, but I understand why it happens.", vocab: [["social pressure", "áp lực xã hội"], ["lose face", "mất mặt"], ["a once-in-a-lifetime event", "sự kiện có một lần trong đời"], ["in debt", "mắc nợ"]] },
  "How important is it to celebrate festivals from other cultures?": { a: "I think it's quite valuable, as long as it's done with respect. Taking part in other cultures' festivals, like Christmas or Diwali, can help people understand different traditions and become more open-minded, which matters a lot in a globalised world. It's also simply fun and a great way to bring communities together. However, there's a risk that people celebrate only the surface, like decorations and parties, without learning what the festival actually means. And I don't think it should come at the expense of our own traditions. Ideally, it should broaden our perspective rather than replace our heritage.", vocab: [["a globalised world", "thế giới toàn cầu hóa"], ["bring communities together", "gắn kết cộng đồng"], ["at the expense of", "đánh đổi bằng, làm tổn hại"]] },
  "Do you think public holidays are good for the economy?": { a: "It's a mixed picture, I'd say. On the one hand, public holidays boost certain sectors, such as tourism, retail and restaurants, because people have time to travel and spend money. In Vietnam, for example, hotels and airlines are often fully booked around national holidays. On the other hand, factories and offices lose working days, which can reduce productivity. However, there's evidence that rested workers are more productive afterwards, so the loss may be smaller than it seems. Overall, I believe a reasonable number of holidays benefits both the economy and people's wellbeing.", vocab: [["a mixed picture", "bức tranh có cả mặt tốt và xấu"], ["boost certain sectors", "thúc đẩy một số ngành"], ["fully booked", "kín chỗ"]] },

  /* ===== Rèn luyện thể chất ===== */
  "How do you usually keep fit?": { a: "My main form of exercise is running. I go for a five-kilometre run around the lake near my house three mornings a week. I also try to build activity into my daily routine, for example by taking the stairs instead of the lift and cycling to class. It's not intense, but it keeps me reasonably fit.", vocab: [["a form of exercise", "hình thức tập luyện"], ["build something into my routine", "đưa điều gì vào thói quen"], ["take the stairs", "đi cầu thang bộ"]] },
  "Do you prefer exercising indoors or outdoors?": { a: "I much prefer exercising outdoors, because I find fresh air and changing scenery far more motivating than staring at a wall in a gym. Running by the lake early in the morning is one of my favourite parts of the day. The only problem is the weather. In the middle of summer or when the air quality is bad, I have no choice but to work out indoors.", vocab: [["changing scenery", "cảnh vật thay đổi"], ["motivating", "tạo động lực"], ["work out", "tập thể dục"]] },
  "Have you ever been a member of a gym?": { a: "Yes, I signed up for a gym membership last year, full of good intentions. For the first month, I went almost every day, but then I got busy with exams and my visits became less and less frequent. In the end, I was basically paying for a gym I never used, so I cancelled it and went back to running.", vocab: [["sign up for", "đăng ký"], ["full of good intentions", "đầy thiện chí, quyết tâm"], ["less and less frequent", "ngày càng thưa thớt"]] },
  "Do you think you get enough exercise these days?": { a: "Probably not quite enough, if I'm honest. I manage to run a few times a week, but I spend most of my day sitting, either in lectures or at my laptop. I often end up with a stiff neck and back. I'd like to add some stretching or yoga to my routine to counteract all that sitting.", vocab: [["a stiff neck", "cứng cổ"], ["add something to my routine", "thêm điều gì vào thói quen"], ["counteract", "bù lại, chống lại tác động"]] },
  "Why do so many people give up on their fitness goals after a short time?": { a: "I think one of the main reasons is that people set unrealistic goals. They start with intense workouts every day and expect quick results, so when they don't see a big change after a few weeks, they lose motivation. Another reason is that exercise is often treated as something extra, rather than part of their routine, so it's the first thing to go when life gets busy. Lack of enjoyment matters too. If you force yourself to do something you find boring, you won't stick with it for long. Finding an activity you genuinely enjoy makes a huge difference.", vocab: [["set unrealistic goals", "đặt mục tiêu phi thực tế"], ["lose motivation", "mất động lực"], ["the first thing to go", "điều đầu tiên bị bỏ đi"], ["stick with it", "kiên trì với nó"]] },
  "Should junk food be taxed more heavily to improve public health?": { a: "I think a moderate tax could help, but it shouldn't be the only measure. Higher prices do tend to reduce consumption, as we've seen with cigarettes and, more recently, sugary drinks in some countries. The extra revenue could also be invested in health campaigns or making fresh food more affordable. However, critics argue that such taxes can be unfair, since poorer families spend a larger proportion of their income on food. People might also just switch to other cheap but unhealthy options. So I'd support a tax, but combined with education and better access to healthy food.", vocab: [["reduce consumption", "giảm tiêu thụ"], ["extra revenue", "nguồn thu thêm"], ["a larger proportion of", "một tỷ lệ lớn hơn của"]] },
  "How has modern working life affected people's physical health?": { a: "In many ways, quite negatively. Most office jobs involve sitting at a desk for eight hours or more, which is linked to back pain, weight gain and even heart disease. Long working hours and commuting also leave people with little time or energy to exercise or cook proper meals, so they often rely on fast food. On top of that, staring at screens all day can cause eye strain and poor sleep. That said, some companies are now trying to address this by offering standing desks, gym subsidies or flexible hours, which is a positive step.", vocab: [["be linked to", "có liên quan đến"], ["eye strain", "mỏi mắt"], ["address this", "giải quyết vấn đề này"], ["a positive step", "một bước tiến tích cực"]] },
  "Do fitness apps and smartwatches really help people stay healthy?": { a: "I think they can, but mostly for people who are already motivated. These devices make it easy to track steps, sleep and heart rate, and seeing your progress can be really encouraging. Some apps also use reminders and challenges, which can turn exercise into a kind of game. My dad, for example, became much more active once he started trying to reach ten thousand steps a day. However, many people stop using them after the novelty wears off. And there's a risk of becoming obsessed with numbers rather than actually listening to your body.", vocab: [["track progress / track steps", "theo dõi tiến độ/số bước"], ["the novelty wears off", "sự mới mẻ qua đi"], ["listen to your body", "lắng nghe cơ thể"]] },

  /* ===== Khoa học & Công nghệ trong nhà ===== */
  "Which household appliance do you find most useful?": { a: "I'd have to say the rice cooker. It might sound boring, but in a Vietnamese home, we eat rice almost every day, and it makes life so much easier. I just put the rice and water in, press a button and forget about it. Mine also has a timer, so dinner can be ready when I get home.", vocab: [["household appliance", "đồ gia dụng"], ["make life easier", "làm cuộc sống dễ dàng hơn"], ["forget about it", "không cần bận tâm nữa"]] },
  "Do you have any smart devices at home?": { a: "Only a few. I have a smart speaker that I mainly use for playing music and setting alarms, and a couple of smart plugs, so I can switch the fan off from my phone. My parents' house has a smart camera at the gate, which they find reassuring. I'm not completely convinced I need more, though.", vocab: [["a smart speaker", "loa thông minh"], ["reassuring", "khiến yên tâm"], ["not completely convinced", "chưa hoàn toàn bị thuyết phục"]] },
  "Are you good at fixing things when they break?": { a: "Not really, to be honest. I can handle simple things, like changing a light bulb or tightening a loose screw, but anything more complicated is beyond me. Usually, I'll watch a YouTube tutorial first and try to fix it myself. If that fails, I call my dad, who's surprisingly handy, or take it to a repair shop.", vocab: [["be beyond me", "vượt quá khả năng của tôi"], ["a loose screw", "con ốc bị lỏng"], ["handy", "khéo tay"]] },
  "Did you enjoy science lessons when you were younger?": { a: "Yes, especially when we got to do experiments. I still remember the excitement of mixing chemicals in the lab and watching them change colour. Theory lessons were less exciting, since we mostly memorised formulas for exams. But I had a brilliant physics teacher who explained things with everyday examples, which made the subject really click for me.", vocab: [["get to do something", "được làm điều gì"], ["memorise formulas", "học thuộc công thức"], ["make something click", "làm điều gì trở nên dễ hiểu"]] },
  "Describe a piece of technology at home that makes your life easier.": { a: "The piece of technology I'd like to talk about is the robot vacuum cleaner we have at home, the small round machine that moves around the floor by itself and cleans up dust and hair.\n\nWe got it about two years ago. Actually, it was a present from my older brother to our parents for their wedding anniversary. At first, my mum was quite sceptical. She said she didn't trust a machine to clean properly, and she was worried it would get stuck under the sofa or fall down the stairs. My dad, on the other hand, was fascinated by it and spent the whole first evening just watching it move around the living room.\n\nThese days, we use it every single day. It's programmed to start automatically at ten in the morning, when everyone is at work or school, so by the time we get home, the floor is clean. Once a week, I empty the dust container and clean the brushes, which only takes a few minutes. We also have an app on our phones, so we can start it or check where it has cleaned even when we're out.\n\nIf we didn't have it, I think our lives would be noticeably more stressful. Our house gets very dusty because it's near a busy road, and we also have a cat that sheds a lot of fur, so before we had the robot, someone had to sweep and mop almost every day. Usually, that someone was my mum, after a long day at work. Now she has more time to relax in the evenings, and there are far fewer arguments about whose turn it is to clean. Even my mum has completely changed her mind and admits it's one of the best gifts she's ever received.", vocab: [["be sceptical", "hoài nghi"], ["be fascinated by", "bị cuốn hút bởi"], ["noticeably more stressful", "căng thẳng hơn rõ rệt"], ["change one's mind", "thay đổi ý kiến"]] },
  "Do labour-saving devices really give people more free time?": { a: "In theory, they should, and to some extent they do. Washing machines and rice cookers, for example, have saved huge amounts of time, particularly for women, who traditionally did most of the housework. However, I'm not convinced that this extra time has turned into real leisure. In many cases, people simply fill it with more work or more tasks, partly because expectations have risen. We now wash clothes far more often than our grandparents did, for instance. And devices like smartphones have arguably made us busier, not freer. So the effect is real, but smaller than we might expect.", vocab: [["labour-saving devices", "thiết bị tiết kiệm sức lao động"], ["in theory", "về lý thuyết"], ["expectations have risen", "kỳ vọng đã tăng lên"]] },
  "What are the privacy risks of having smart devices in the home?": { a: "There are quite a few, I think. Smart speakers and cameras are constantly collecting data, sometimes including recordings of private conversations, and users often have no idea how that information is stored or who has access to it. Companies may use it for targeted advertising, or share it with third parties. There's also the risk of hacking. If a smart camera or door lock is poorly protected, criminals could potentially spy on a household or even get inside. That's why I believe people should change default passwords, check privacy settings and think carefully about which devices they really need.", vocab: [["collect data", "thu thập dữ liệu"], ["targeted advertising", "quảng cáo nhắm mục tiêu"], ["third parties", "bên thứ ba"], ["spy on", "do thám, theo dõi"]] },
  "Why are older people sometimes reluctant to use new technology?": { a: "I think there are several reasons. Many older people didn't grow up with digital devices, so things that seem intuitive to younger generations, like swiping or using icons, can feel confusing. They may also be afraid of making mistakes, for example pressing the wrong button and losing money in a banking app. Sometimes it's simply a question of not seeing the point, if their old way of doing things still works. My grandmother, for instance, refused to use video calls until she realised she could see her grandchildren abroad. So patient support and clear benefits make a big difference.", vocab: [["be reluctant to", "ngần ngại, miễn cưỡng"], ["intuitive", "trực quan, dễ hiểu"], ["not see the point", "không thấy ý nghĩa, lợi ích"]] },
  "How might homes of the future be different from the ones we live in today?": { a: "I imagine they'll be far more automated and energy-efficient. Homes might generate their own electricity from solar panels and use smart systems to adjust lighting and temperature automatically, which would cut energy bills. Robots could take over more household chores, like cooking and laundry. As cities become more crowded, homes may also get smaller but more flexible, with furniture that folds away or rooms that change function. That said, I suspect many people will still want some traditional elements, like natural materials and green spaces, because technology alone doesn't make a house feel like a home.", vocab: [["energy-efficient", "tiết kiệm năng lượng"], ["take over", "đảm nhận, thay thế"], ["household chores", "việc nhà"], ["fold away", "gấp gọn lại"]] },
}); })(typeof self !== 'undefined' ? self : this);
